/* notes for mtai - generated by tools/build_site.py */
window.COURSE.notes = window.COURSE.notes || {};
window.COURSE.notes["mtai"] = [{"n": 1, "title": "Vectors, Matrices & Linear Systems", "tagline": "AI models never see \"a photo\" or \"a sentence\". They see lists of numbers.", "minutes": 21, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>AI models never see \"a photo\" or \"a sentence\". They see <strong>lists of numbers</strong>. Linear algebra is the toolkit for handling lists of numbers (vectors) and tables of numbers (matrices).</p>\n<p>This module answers five questions:</p>\n<ol><li>How do we store one data point? → <strong>vector</strong></li><li>How do we compare two data points? → <strong>dot product, norm, cosine similarity</strong></li><li>How do we store a whole dataset and transform it? → <strong>matrix</strong></li><li>How do we solve for unknowns? → <strong>Ax = b, Gaussian elimination, inverse</strong></li><li>How much real information is in the data? → <strong>rank, basis, projection, least squares</strong></li></ol>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:</strong> school algebra, solving 2 equations in 2 unknowns, basic trigonometry (cos of an angle).</p>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.1", "title": "Vectors as data", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A vector is just an ordered list of numbers. Think of it as a <strong>row in your Excel sheet</strong>: one student = (marks in maths, marks in physics, attendance %).</p>\n<p>In R<sup>n</sup> (read \"R-n\"), n is the number of entries. R<sup>2</sup> is the flat plane, R<sup>3</sup> is 3D space, R<sup>1000</sup> is a 1000-feature data point (we cannot draw it, but the maths works the same).</p>\n<h4>Notation</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[v = (v_1, v_2, \\ldots, v_n) \\in \\mathbb{R}^n, \\qquad \\text{e.g.}\\quad v = (2,3) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2\\]</div></div>\n<h4>Geometric meaning</h4>\n<p>A vector is an <strong>arrow from the origin to a point</strong>. (2, 3) means: go 2 right, 3 up. It has a <strong>length</strong> and a <strong>direction</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Feature vector (the AI meaning)</h4>\n<p>A data point = a vector of its features.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[x = \\big(1200\\ (\\text{area}),\\ 3\\ (\\text{bedrooms}),\\ 10\\ (\\text{age})\\big)\\]</div></div>\n<p>A grayscale image of 28x28 pixels = a vector in R<sup>784</sup> (flatten the pixels). A word embedding = a vector in R<sup>300</sup> or more.</p>\n<h4>Vector operations</h4>\n<p><strong>Addition</strong> – add entry by entry.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[(1,2) + (3,4) = (1+3,\\ 2+4) = (4,6)\\]</div></div>\n<p>Geometry: place the second arrow at the tip of the first (tip-to-tail).</p>\n<p><strong>Scalar multiplication</strong> – multiply every entry by a number c.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[3\\,(1,2) = (3,6)\\]</div></div>\n<p>Geometry: stretches the arrow by c. If c is negative the arrow flips direction.</p>\n<p><strong>Subtraction</strong> – a - b = a + (-1)b. Geometry: arrow from tip of b to tip of a.</p>\n<h4>Linear combination</h4>\n<p>Mix vectors using scalars and add:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[c_1v_1 + c_2v_2 + \\cdots + c_kv_k \\;=\\; \\sum_{i=1}^{k} c_i v_i\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: 2\\<em>(1, 0) + 3\\</em>(0, 1) = (2, 3). Any point in the plane can be built from (1,0) and (0,1) like this.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it matters:</strong> a neural-network neuron computes w1<em>x1 + w2</em>x2 + ... = a linear combination of the inputs. A linear combination is the most basic move in AI.</p>\n<h4>Common traps</h4>\n<ul><li>You can only add vectors of the <strong>same size</strong>.</li><li>Vector (1,2) is not the same as (2,1): <strong>order matters</strong>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.2", "title": "Dot product, norms and distance", "html": "<h4>Dot product</h4>\n<p><strong>Intuition:</strong> multiply matching entries and add. It measures \"how much two vectors point the same way\".</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[a\\cdot b = a_1b_1 + a_2b_2 + \\cdots + a_nb_n \\;=\\; \\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_i b_i\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: a = (1, 2, 3), b = (4, 5, 6)</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[a\\cdot b = 1\\cdot 4 + 2\\cdot 5 + 3\\cdot 6 = 4 + 10 + 18 = 32\\]</div></div>\n<p><strong>Use when:</strong> you need a similarity score, a projection, or an angle.</p>\n<h4>Length (norm)</h4>\n<p><strong>Intuition:</strong> distance from origin = Pythagoras.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\|a\\| = \\sqrt{a_1^2 + a_2^2 + \\cdots + a_n^2} = \\sqrt{a\\cdot a}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: ||(3, 4)|| = sqrt(9 + 16) = 5.</p>\n<p>A <strong>unit vector</strong> has length 1. Make any vector a unit vector by dividing by its length: a / ||a||. (Called normalising.)</p>\n<p>Other norms used in AI:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{gathered}\\|a\\|_1 = |a_1| + |a_2| + \\cdots + |a_n| \\qquad (\\text{Manhattan distance, used in Lasso})\\\\[4pt] \\|a\\|_2 = \\sqrt{a_1^2 + \\cdots + a_n^2} \\qquad (\\text{usual length, used in Ridge})\\end{gathered}\\]</div></div>\n<h4>Distance between two data points</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[d(a,b) = \\|a-b\\| = \\sqrt{(a_1-b_1)^2 + \\cdots + (a_n-b_n)^2}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: d((1,2), (4,6)) = sqrt(9 + 16) = 5. <strong>Use when:</strong> k-nearest neighbours, k-means clustering.</p>\n<h4>Angle between vectors</h4>\n<p>The key link between dot product and angle:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[a\\cdot b = \\|a\\|\\,\\|b\\|\\cos\\theta\\]</div></div>\n<p>So</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\cos\\theta = \\frac{a\\cdot b}{\\|a\\|\\,\\|b\\|}\\]</div></div>\n<ul><li>a . b &gt; 0: angle less than 90 degrees (similar direction)</li><li>a . b = 0: exactly 90 degrees (<strong>orthogonal</strong>, no similarity)</li><li>a . b &lt; 0: angle more than 90 degrees (opposite-ish)</li></ul>\n<h4>Cosine similarity</h4>\n<p>The cosine formula above, used as a similarity score between -1 and 1.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\cos_{\\text{sim}}(a,b) = \\frac{a\\cdot b}{\\|a\\|\\,\\|b\\|}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: a = (1, 0), b = (1, 1)</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{gathered}a\\cdot b = 1,\\quad \\|a\\| = 1,\\quad \\|b\\| = \\sqrt{2}\\\\[4pt] \\cos_{\\text{sim}} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 0.707 \\qquad (\\theta = 45^\\circ)\\end{gathered}\\]</div></div>\n<h4>Why these measure similarity in AI</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Distance</strong> says how far apart two points are: small distance = similar.</li><li><strong>Cosine similarity</strong> ignores length and only checks direction. Perfect for text: a long and a short document on the same topic point the same way even though their word counts differ.</li><li>Search engines, recommender systems and chatbots' embeddings all rank things by cosine similarity or dot product.</li></ul>\n<h4>Common traps</h4>\n<ul><li>Cosine similarity is undefined if a vector is the zero vector (division by zero).</li><li>Distance is affected by feature scales (marks 0-100 vs salary in lakhs), so <strong>normalise features</strong> first.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.3", "title": "Matrices as data and as transformations", "html": "<h4>What is a matrix?</h4>\n<p>A rectangular table of numbers with m rows and n columns (an m x n matrix).</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 6 \\end{pmatrix} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{2\\times 3}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Entry a<sub>ij</sub> = row i, column j.</p>\n<h4>A dataset as a matrix</h4>\n<p>Stack data points as rows, features as columns.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[X = \\left(\\begin{array}{r|ccc} & \\text{area} & \\text{bedrooms} & \\text{age} \\\\ \\hline \\text{house 1} & 1200 & 3 & 10 \\\\ \\text{house 2} & 800 & 2 & 5 \\\\ \\text{house 3} & 1500 & 4 & 20 \\end{array}\\right)\\]</div></div>\n<p>This is the X matrix (n samples x d features). Your Excel sheet <strong>is</strong> a matrix.</p>\n<h4>Matrix-vector multiplication</h4>\n<p><strong>Method 1 (row view):</strong> each output entry = dot product of a row with the vector.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1\\cdot 5 + 2\\cdot 6 \\\\ 3\\cdot 5 + 4\\cdot 6 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 17 \\\\ 39 \\end{pmatrix}\\]</div></div>\n<p><strong>Method 2 (column view, more powerful):</strong> Ax = a linear combination of the columns of A, with weights from x.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix} = 5\\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 3 \\end{pmatrix} + 6\\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\\\ 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 17 \\\\ 39 \\end{pmatrix}\\]</div></div>\n<p><strong>Size rule:</strong> (m x n) times (n x 1) gives (m x 1). The inner numbers must match.</p>\n<p><strong>Meaning:</strong> predictions of a linear model are y = Xw. Each house's price = dot product of its features with the weights.</p>\n<h4>Matrix-matrix multiplication</h4>\n<p>(m x n) times (n x p) gives (m x p). Entry (i, j) = row i of A dot column j of B.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1\\cdot 0 + 2\\cdot 3 & 1\\cdot 1 + 2\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3\\cdot 0 + 4\\cdot 3 & 3\\cdot 1 + 4\\cdot 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 6 & 5 \\\\ 12 & 11 \\end{pmatrix}\\]</div></div>\n<p><strong>Meaning:</strong> doing one transformation after another (see 1.4). Each layer of a neural network is a matrix multiplication.</p>\n<h4>Rules to remember</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{array}{l}AB \\ne BA \\quad \\text{in general (order matters!)}\\\\[3pt] (AB)C = A(BC) \\quad \\text{(associative)}\\\\[3pt] A(B+C) = AB + AC \\quad \\text{(distributive)}\\\\[3pt] (AB)^{\\mathsf T} = B^{\\mathsf T}A^{\\mathsf T} \\quad \\text{(transpose reverses order)}\\\\[3pt] AI = IA = A \\quad \\text{(identity)}\\end{array}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Transpose A<sup>T</sup>: swap rows and columns.</p>\n<h4>Common traps</h4>\n<ul><li>Checking sizes first saves you: if inner dimensions do not match, the product does not exist.</li><li>Never assume AB = BA.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.4", "title": "Linear transformations", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A matrix is a <strong>machine</strong> that takes a vector in and gives a vector out, by reshaping space (rotating, stretching, squashing). A transformation T is <strong>linear</strong> if</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[T(u+v) = T(u) + T(v) \\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad T(cu) = c\\,T(u)\\]</div></div>\n<p>Grid lines stay straight and the origin stays fixed.</p>\n<h4>The secret: columns tell the story</h4>\n<p>The columns of A are where the basis vectors (1,0) and (0,1) land. So to build a matrix for a transformation, track where e1 = (1,0) and e2 = (0,1) go and write them as columns.</p>\n<h4>Standard 2D examples</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{array}{l}\\text{Scaling (by } a \\text{ in } x,\\ b \\text{ in } y\\text{):}\\quad \\begin{pmatrix} a & 0 \\\\ 0 & b \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[6pt] \\text{Rotation by } \\theta\\text{:}\\quad \\begin{pmatrix} \\cos\\theta & -\\sin\\theta \\\\ \\sin\\theta & \\cos\\theta \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[6pt] \\text{Projection onto } x\\text{-axis:}\\quad \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[6pt] \\text{Reflection about } x\\text{-axis:}\\quad \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}\\end{array}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: rotate (1, 0) by 90 degrees. cos90 = 0, sin90 = 1.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad (1,0) \\mapsto (0,1)\\]</div></div>\n<h4>Composition</h4>\n<p>Applying T1 then T2 equals multiplying matrices <strong>right to left</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[T_2(T_1(x)) = (A_2A_1)\\,x\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: scale by 2 (S = 2I) then rotate 90 degrees (R). Combined matrix = R S. Since the order changes the result (rotate-then-shear is not shear-then-rotate), AB ≠ BA now has a geometric reason.</p>\n<h4>AI meaning</h4>\n<p>A neural-network layer = linear transformation (matrix W) followed by a nonlinearity. Stacking layers = composing transformations. Without the nonlinearity, many layers would collapse into one matrix (A2 A1 is just another matrix).</p>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.5", "title": "Systems of linear equations", "html": "<h4>Representation Ax = b</h4>\n<p>Two equations, two unknowns:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\left\\{ \\begin{aligned} 2x + y &= 5 \\\\ x - y &= 1 \\end{aligned} \\right.\\]</div></div>\n<p>Write as</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\underbrace{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\end{pmatrix}}_{A} \\underbrace{\\begin{pmatrix} x \\\\ y \\end{pmatrix}}_{x} = \\underbrace{\\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 1 \\end{pmatrix}}_{b} \\qquad \\text{i.e. } Ax = b\\]</div></div>\n<p>A = coefficient matrix, x = unknowns, b = right-hand side. Column view: \"is b a linear combination of the columns of A?\"</p>\n<h4>Gaussian elimination (step by step)</h4>\n<p><strong>Idea:</strong> use row operations to make the system triangular, then solve from the bottom up. Allowed operations:</p>\n<ol><li>Swap two rows</li><li>Multiply a row by a non-zero number</li><li>Add a multiple of one row to another</li></ol>\n<p>These do not change the solution.</p>\n<p><strong>Augmented matrix</strong> \\([A \\mid b]\\):</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[[A \\mid b] = \\left(\\begin{array}{cc|c} 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\end{array}\\right)\\]</div></div>\n<p>Step 1: R2 &lt;- R2 - (1/2) R1</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\left(\\begin{array}{cc|c} 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\end{array}\\right) \\xrightarrow{\\;R_2 \\leftarrow R_2 - \\frac{1}{2}R_1\\;} \\left(\\begin{array}{cc|c} 2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2} \\end{array}\\right)\\]</div></div>\n<p>Step 2: back substitution</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[-\\tfrac{3}{2}y = -\\tfrac{3}{2} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; y = 1, \\qquad 2x + 1 = 5 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; x = 2\\]</div></div>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> 2(2) + 1 = 5 and 2 - 1 = 1. Both hold.</p>\n<p><strong>Reduced row echelon form (Gauss-Jordan):</strong> keep going until the left side is the identity. The right side is then the answer directly.</p>\n<h4>Existence and uniqueness of solutions</h4>\n<p>After elimination, look at the rows:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Unique solution:</strong> every variable has a pivot (leading non-zero entry). Example above.</li><li><strong>No solution (inconsistent):</strong> a row like <code>[0 0 | 5]</code> (means 0 = 5, impossible). Geometrically: parallel lines.</li><li><strong>Infinitely many solutions:</strong> a free variable exists (a column without a pivot) and no contradiction. Geometrically: same line or plane.</li></ul>\n<p>Quick test with rank (see 1.8):</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{array}{l}\\operatorname{rank}(A) = \\operatorname{rank}([A\\mid b]) = n & \\Rightarrow \\text{unique solution}\\\\[3pt] \\operatorname{rank}(A) = \\operatorname{rank}([A\\mid b]) < n & \\Rightarrow \\text{infinitely many solutions}\\\\[3pt] \\operatorname{rank}(A) < \\operatorname{rank}([A\\mid b]) & \\Rightarrow \\text{no solution}\\end{array}\\]</div></div>\n<p>(n = number of unknowns.)</p>\n<h4>Example with no solution</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\left\\{ \\begin{aligned} x + y &= 2 \\\\ x + y &= 5 \\end{aligned} \\right.\\]</div></div>\n<p>R2 - R1 gives 0 = 3. Impossible, so no solution.</p>\n<h4>Common traps</h4>\n<ul><li>Apply every row operation to the <strong>whole row including b</strong>.</li><li>Do not divide by zero: swap rows to get a non-zero pivot.</li><li>In AI, real data usually gives <strong>more equations than unknowns</strong> (no exact solution), which is why we need least squares (1.9).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.6", "title": "Matrix inverse and determinant", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>The inverse A<sup>−1</sup> is the <strong>undo button</strong> of a matrix. If A rotates by 30 degrees, A<sup>−1</sup> rotates back by 30 degrees.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[AA^{-1} = A^{-1}A = I\\]</div></div>\n<p>If A is invertible, Ax = b has the solution x = A<sup>−1</sup> b.</p>\n<h4>Determinant (meaning)</h4>\n<p>For a square matrix, det(A) is the <strong>factor by which A scales area (2D) or volume (3D)</strong>. If det = 0, space gets squashed flat (a plane collapses to a line), information is lost, and you cannot undo it.</p>\n<h4>2 x 2 computation</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[A = \\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\det(A) = ad - bc, \\quad A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{ad-bc}\\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Memory trick: swap the diagonal entries, flip the signs of the off-diagonal ones, divide by the determinant.</p>\n<p>Example: A = \\(\\begin{pmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{pmatrix}\\)</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{gathered}\\det\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 3 \\end{pmatrix} = 2\\cdot 3 - 1\\cdot 5 = 1\\\\[5pt] A^{-1} = \\frac{1}{1}\\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[5pt] AA^{-1} = \\begin{pmatrix} 2\\cdot 3 + 1\\cdot(-5) & 2\\cdot(-1) + 1\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5\\cdot 3 + 3\\cdot(-5) & 5\\cdot(-1) + 3\\cdot 2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} \\;\\checkmark\\end{gathered}\\]</div></div>\n<h4>3 x 3 determinant (expand along first row)</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\\\ d & e & f \\\\ g & h & i \\end{vmatrix} = a(ei - fh) - b(di - fg) + c(dh - eg)\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: det \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\)</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{gathered}\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0 \\end{vmatrix} = 1(1\\cdot 0 - 4\\cdot 6) - 2(0\\cdot 0 - 4\\cdot 5) + 3(0\\cdot 6 - 1\\cdot 5)\\\\[4pt] = -24 + 40 - 15 = 1\\end{gathered}\\]</div></div>\n<h4>When does an inverse exist?</h4>\n<p>A square matrix A is invertible when ALL of these are true (they are equivalent):</p>\n<ul><li>det(A) ≠ 0</li><li>rank(A) = n (full rank)</li><li>columns are linearly independent</li><li>Ax = 0 has only the solution x = 0</li><li>Ax = b has exactly one solution for every b</li></ul>\n<p>Otherwise A is <strong>singular</strong> (no inverse).</p>\n<h4>Useful rules</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[(AB)^{-1} = B^{-1}A^{-1}, \\quad \\det(AB) = \\det(A)\\det(B), \\quad \\det(A^{\\mathsf T}) = \\det(A)\\]</div></div>\n<h4>Common traps</h4>\n<ul><li>Only <strong>square</strong> matrices have inverses (and not all of them).</li><li>In practice we avoid computing A<sup>−1</sup> on computers; we solve Ax = b by elimination because it is faster and more accurate.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.7", "title": "Vector spaces (applied view)", "html": "<h4>Intuition</h4>\n<p>A <strong>vector space</strong> is a collection of vectors where you can add them and scale them and <strong>never leave the collection</strong>. R<sup>2</sup> and R<sup>3</sup> are the standard examples. The rules are just the usual algebra: closure under addition and scalar multiplication, a zero vector, and negatives.</p>\n<h4>Subspace</h4>\n<p>A subspace is a vector space living <strong>inside</strong> a bigger one. A set W inside R<sup>n</sup> is a subspace if:</p>\n<ol><li>It contains the zero vector.</li><li>u, v in W  ⇒  u + v in W.</li><li>c in R, u in W  ⇒  c\\*u in W.</li></ol>\n<p>Examples in R<sup>3</sup>: a line through the origin, a plane through the origin. A plane that does <strong>not</strong> pass through the origin is NOT a subspace (fails rule 1).</p>\n<p>Worked check: W = { (x, y) : y = 2x }. Zero vector (0,0) is in W. (1,2) + (3,6) = (4,8) has 8 = 2<em>4, so it stays in. c</em>(1,2) = (c, 2c) stays in. So W is a subspace (a line through the origin).</p>\n<h4>Span</h4>\n<p>The span of vectors v1, ..., vk = <strong>all possible linear combinations</strong> of them.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\operatorname{span}\\{v_1, \\ldots, v_k\\} = \\{\\, c_1v_1 + \\cdots + c_kv_k \\;:\\; c_1, \\ldots, c_k \\in \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}\\]</div></div>\n<ul><li>span of one non-zero vector in R<sup>2</sup> = a line</li><li>span of two non-parallel vectors in R<sup>2</sup> = the whole plane</li></ul>\n<p>To test if b is in the span of the columns of A: check if Ax = b has a solution.</p>\n<h4>Linear independence</h4>\n<p><strong>Intuition:</strong> vectors are independent if <strong>none of them is redundant</strong>, i.e. none can be built from the others.</p>\n<p>Formal test: v1, ..., vk are independent if</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[c_1v_1 + \\cdots + c_kv_k = 0 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; c_1 = c_2 = \\cdots = c_k = 0\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: (1,2) and (2,4): 2\\<em>(1,2) - 1\\</em>(2,4) = 0 with non-zero coefficients, so <strong>dependent</strong>. Example: (1,0) and (0,1): c1\\<em>(1,0) + c2\\</em>(0,1) = (c1, c2) = 0 means c1 = c2 = 0, so <strong>independent</strong>.</p>\n<p>How to test in practice: put vectors as columns of a matrix, row-reduce. If every column has a pivot, they are independent.</p>\n<p>Shortcuts: more than n vectors in R<sup>n</sup> are always dependent. Any set containing the zero vector is dependent.</p>\n<h4>Basis and dimension</h4>\n<p>A <strong>basis</strong> = a set of vectors that is (1) independent and (2) spans the space. It is the <strong>smallest complete set of directions</strong>: every vector in the space can be written in exactly one way as a combination of basis vectors.</p>\n<p>Standard basis of R<sup>3</sup>: e1 = (1,0,0), e2 = (0,1,0), e3 = (0,0,1).</p>\n<p><strong>Dimension</strong> = number of vectors in a basis (all bases of a space have the same size). dim R<sup>n</sup> = n. A line has dimension 1, a plane has dimension 2.</p>\n<p><strong>Coordinates:</strong> if b = c1<em>v1 + c2</em>v2 in basis {v1, v2}, then (c1, c2) are the coordinates of b in that basis.</p>\n<h4>AI meaning</h4>\n<p>If two features are dependent (for example height in cm and height in inches), one is redundant. The independent directions are the real information. PCA finds a better basis for your data.</p>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.8", "title": "Rank and the column / row space", "html": "<h4>Column space and row space</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{array}{l}\\operatorname{Col}(A) = \\operatorname{span}\\{\\text{columns of } A\\} = \\{Ax\\} \\quad \\text{(all possible outputs)}\\\\[4pt] \\operatorname{Row}(A) = \\operatorname{span}\\{\\text{rows of } A\\}\\end{array}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Intuition: Col(A) is the set of all b for which Ax = b is solvable.</p>\n<h4>Rank</h4>\n<p><strong>Rank = number of independent columns = number of pivots after Gaussian elimination = dim Col(A) = dim Row(A).</strong></p>\n<p>Key fact: <strong>row rank = column rank</strong>, always.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 9 \\end{pmatrix} \\xrightarrow{\\;R_2 \\leftarrow R_2 - 2R_1,\\; R_3 \\leftarrow R_3 - 3R_1\\;} \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad \\operatorname{rank}(A) = 1\\]</div></div>\n<p>All rows are multiples of one row, so the data really lives on a line.</p>\n<h4>Useful facts</h4>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{array}{l}\\operatorname{rank}(A) \\le \\min(m,n) \\quad (A \\text{ is } m\\times n)\\\\[3pt] \\text{full rank: } \\operatorname{rank}(A) = \\min(m,n)\\\\[3pt] \\operatorname{rank}(A) + \\dim\\operatorname{Null}(A) = n \\quad (\\text{rank–nullity, } n = \\text{number of columns})\\end{array}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Null space Null(A) = all x with Ax = 0. Example above: n = 3, rank = 1, so dim Null(A) = 2.</p>\n<h4>Dimensionality of data</h4>\n<ul><li>A dataset with 1000 features but rank 20 really lives in a <strong>20-dimensional</strong> world; the other directions are redundant.</li><li>Low rank = compressible. This is the idea behind PCA, SVD, recommender systems (low-rank user-item matrices) and LoRA fine-tuning.</li><li>Rank deficiency (dependent features) is called <strong>multicollinearity</strong> and makes regression unstable.</li></ul>\n<h4>Common traps</h4>\n<ul><li>Rank is counted from the pivots, not from the number of non-zero rows before elimination.</li><li>rank(A) = rank(A<sup>T</sup>).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.9", "title": "Orthogonality and orthonormal bases", "html": "<h4>Orthogonality</h4>\n<p>Two vectors are <strong>orthogonal</strong> (perpendicular) when</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[u\\cdot v = 0\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: (1, 2) . (2, -1) = 2 - 2 = 0, so they are orthogonal.</p>\n<p>If a vector is orthogonal to every vector in a subspace, it is orthogonal to that subspace.</p>\n<h4>Orthonormal basis</h4>\n<p>Orthogonal + every vector has length 1:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[q_i\\cdot q_j = 0 \\;\\; (i \\ne j), \\qquad q_i\\cdot q_i = 1\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: (1/sqrt2, 1/sqrt2) and (1/sqrt2, -1/sqrt2) form an orthonormal basis of R<sup>2</sup>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why it is great:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Coordinates are just dot products: if b = c1<em>q1 + c2</em>q2 then ci = qi . b. No equation-solving needed.</li><li>For a matrix Q with orthonormal columns: <strong>Q<sup>T</sup> Q = I</strong>. For a square one, Q<sup>−1</sup> = Q<sup>T</sup> (the inverse is free!).</li><li>Rotations keep lengths and angles unchanged.</li></ul>\n<p>(Gram-Schmidt turns any basis into an orthonormal one; the idea: subtract from each new vector its projections onto the earlier ones, then normalise.)</p>\n<h4>Projection onto a line</h4>\n<p>Project b onto the direction of a:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\operatorname{proj}_{a}(b) = \\frac{a\\cdot b}{a\\cdot a}\\,a\\]</div></div>\n<p>Example: b = (3, 4), a = (1, 0). a . b = 3, a . a = 1, so proj = 3\\*(1,0) = (3, 0). The shadow of b on the x-axis.</p>\n<p>The leftover <strong>error</strong> e = b - proj is orthogonal to a.</p>\n<h4>Projection onto a subspace</h4>\n<p>Let the columns of A span the subspace (A has independent columns). Projection of b onto Col(A):</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{array}{l}x = (A^{\\mathsf T}A)^{-1}A^{\\mathsf T}b \\quad \\text{(best coefficients)}\\\\[3pt] p = Ax \\quad \\text{(the projection)}\\\\[3pt] P = A(A^{\\mathsf T}A)^{-1}A^{\\mathsf T} \\quad \\text{(projection matrix)}\\end{array}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Key property: the error b - p is orthogonal to every column of A, i.e. A<sup>T</sup> (b - A x<sub>hat</sub>) = 0.</p>\n<h4>Least squares (why projection matters)</h4>\n<p>Problem: Ax = b has <strong>no exact solution</strong> (more equations than unknowns; b is not in Col(A)). We pick the x that makes Ax <strong>closest</strong> to b, minimising ||Ax - b||<sup>2</sup>.</p>\n<p>Solution: project b onto Col(A). This gives the <strong>normal equations</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[A^{\\mathsf T}A\\,x = A^{\\mathsf T}b\\]</div></div>\n<p><strong>Worked example:</strong> fit a line y = c + d\\*t through points (t, y) = (0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 2).</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\begin{array}{l}A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad b = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2 \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[8pt] A^{\\mathsf T}A = \\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5 \\end{pmatrix}, \\quad A^{\\mathsf T}b = \\begin{pmatrix} 5 \\\\ 6 \\end{pmatrix}\\\\[8pt] 3c + 3d = 5,\\quad 3c + 5d = 6 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; d = \\tfrac{1}{2},\\ c = \\tfrac{7}{6}\\end{array}\\]</div></div>\n<p>Best line: y = 7/6 + (1/2) t. Check: predictions = 1.167, 1.667, 2.167; errors = -0.167, 0.333, -0.167. Sum of errors = 0 and errors are orthogonal to the columns of A (sum of errors \\<em> t = 0\\</em>(-0.167) + 1<em>0.333 + 2</em>(-0.167) = 0).</p>\n<h4>AI meaning</h4>\n<p>Linear regression <strong>is</strong> least squares: weights w = (X<sup>T</sup> X)<sup>−1</sup> X<sup>T</sup> y. Training is projecting the target vector onto the space spanned by the features.</p>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.10", "title": "AI/ML meaning throughout", "html": "<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Maths idea</th><th>What it is in AI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Vector</td><td>One data point (feature vector), an embedding, a weight vector</td></tr><tr><td>Matrix</td><td>A whole dataset X (samples x features), or a layer's weights W</td></tr><tr><td>Dot product</td><td>Similarity, a neuron's weighted sum, attention scores</td></tr><tr><td>Norm / distance</td><td>Error size, k-NN, clustering, regularisation (L1, L2)</td></tr><tr><td>Cosine similarity</td><td>Comparing text/image embeddings, semantic search</td></tr><tr><td>Matrix multiplication</td><td>A forward pass through a layer</td></tr><tr><td>Linear transformation</td><td>What each layer does before the nonlinearity</td></tr><tr><td>Ax = b</td><td>Linear models, systems that must be solved</td></tr><tr><td>Inverse / determinant</td><td>Closed-form solutions, whether a transformation loses information</td></tr><tr><td>Span, basis</td><td>The directions your data actually uses; feature space</td></tr><tr><td>Rank</td><td>True dimensionality of data; low-rank compression (PCA, SVD, LoRA)</td></tr><tr><td>Orthogonality</td><td>Independent features, PCA components, stable numerics</td></tr><tr><td>Projection / least squares</td><td>Linear regression, fitting models to noisy data</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Why linear algebra is the language of AI:</strong> data = vectors/matrices, models = linear maps plus simple nonlinearities, and learning = solving or approximating equations like Ax = b. GPUs are fast mainly because they multiply matrices fast.</p>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>I need to...</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Combine features with weights</td><td>w . x (dot product)</td></tr><tr><td>Find length of a vector</td><td>sqrt(a . a)</td></tr><tr><td>Measure how far apart two points are</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Measure similarity ignoring size</td><td>(a . b) / (</td></tr><tr><td>Check if two vectors are perpendicular</td><td>a . b = 0</td></tr><tr><td>Apply a transformation</td><td>A x</td></tr><tr><td>Do transformation 1 then 2</td><td>A2 A1</td></tr><tr><td>Solve Ax = b</td><td>Gaussian elimination</td></tr><tr><td>Check if a square matrix is invertible</td><td>det(A) ≠ 0</td></tr><tr><td>Invert a 2x2 matrix</td><td>(1/(ad-bc)) \\(\\begin{pmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{pmatrix}\\)</td></tr><tr><td>Check independence</td><td>Row-reduce: pivot in every column?</td></tr><tr><td>Count real dimensions of data</td><td>rank(A)</td></tr><tr><td>Project b on a line</td><td>((a . b)/(a . a)) a</td></tr><tr><td>Best fit when no exact solution</td><td>A<sup>T</sup> A x = A<sup>T</sup> b</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<ul><li>Sizes must match before multiplying; AB and BA are different.</li><li>Always apply row operations to the full augmented row.</li><li>Zero determinant means no inverse. Do not divide by it.</li><li>A plane not through the origin is not a subspace.</li><li>A set of vectors containing the zero vector is always dependent.</li><li>Cosine similarity of the zero vector is undefined.</li><li>Normalise features before using distances.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul><li>Vector = list of numbers = arrow = data point. Linear combination = c1 v1 + c2 v2 + ...</li><li>a . b = sum of products = ||a|| ||b|| cos(theta). Zero means perpendicular.</li><li>cos<sub>sim</sub> = (a . b) / (||a|| ||b||), between -1 and 1.</li><li>Matrix = dataset or transformation. Columns of A show where basis vectors land.</li><li>(m x n)(n x p) = (m x p). AB ≠ BA. (AB)<sup>T</sup> = B<sup>T</sup> A<sup>T</sup>. (AB)<sup>−1</sup> = B<sup>−1</sup> A<sup>−1</sup>.</li><li>Gaussian elimination: row-reduce \\([A \\mid b]\\). Unique / none / infinite from pivots.</li><li>det = area scale factor. det = 0 means singular, no inverse.</li><li>Subspace: has 0, closed under + and scalar multiples. Span = all combinations. Basis = independent + spanning. Dimension = size of basis.</li><li>Rank = number of pivots = dim Col(A). Rank-nullity: rank + nullity = n.</li><li>Orthonormal: qi . qj = 0, qi . qi = 1, so Q<sup>T</sup> Q = I.</li><li>Least squares: A<sup>T</sup> A x = A<sup>T</sup> b. Error is orthogonal to Col(A).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"divider\"></div>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Let a = (2, -1, 3), b = (1, 4, 0). Find a . b, ||a||, and cos(theta) between a and b.", "a": "<p>a.b = 2 - 4 + 0 = -2; ||a|| = sqrt14.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Let a = (2, -1, 3), b = (1, 4, 0). Find a . b, ||a||, and cos(theta) between a and b."}, {"q": "Compute the distance between (1, 2, 3) and (4, 6, 15).", "a": "<p>sqrt(9+16+144) = 13.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Compute the distance between (1, 2, 3) and (4, 6, 15)."}, {"q": "Multiply \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\) by \\(\\begin{pmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}\\). Then check whether AB = BA.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Multiply \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\) by \\(\\begin{pmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}\\). Then check whether AB = BA."}, {"q": "Write the 2 x 2 matrix that rotates vectors by 90 degrees. Apply it to (2, 3).", "a": "<p>\\(\\begin{pmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\), result (-3, 2).</p>", "tag": "easy", "qHtml": "Write the 2 x 2 matrix that rotates vectors by 90 degrees. Apply it to (2, 3)."}, {"q": "Solve by Gaussian elimination: x + 2y + z = 4, 2x + y - z = 1, x - y + 2z = 3.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Solve by Gaussian elimination: x + 2y + z = 4, 2x + y - z = 1, x - y + 2z = 3."}, {"q": "For A = \\(\\begin{pmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{pmatrix}\\), find det(A) and A<sup>−1</sup>. Verify A A<sup>−1</sup> = I.", "a": "<p>det = 10, inverse = (1/10)\\(\\begin{pmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}\\).</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "For A = \\(\\begin{pmatrix}4 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{pmatrix}\\), find det(A) and A<sup>−1</sup>. Verify A A<sup>−1</sup> = I."}, {"q": "Is {(1, 2, 3), (2, 4, 6), (1, 0, 1)} linearly independent? Justify.", "a": "<p>dependent (second = 2 x first).</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Is {(1, 2, 3), (2, 4, 6), (1, 0, 1)} linearly independent? Justify."}, {"q": "Find the rank of \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and the dimension of its null space.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "medium", "qHtml": "Find the rank of \\(\\begin{pmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{pmatrix}\\) and the dimension of its null space."}, {"q": "Check whether W = { (x, y, z) : x + y + z = 0 } is a subspace of R<sup>3</sup> and find a basis.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "hard", "qHtml": "Check whether W = { (x, y, z) : x + y + z = 0 } is a subspace of R<sup>3</sup> and find a basis."}, {"q": "Project b = (2, 3) onto a = (1, 1). Verify the error is orthogonal to a.", "a": "<p>proj = (2.5, 2.5).</p>", "tag": "hard", "qHtml": "Project b = (2, 3) onto a = (1, 1). Verify the error is orthogonal to a."}, {"q": "Fit a line y = c + d\\*t to (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 4) using the normal equations.", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "hard", "qHtml": "Fit a line y = c + d\\*t to (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 4) using the normal equations."}, {"q": "Explain in 2-3 lines why cosine similarity is preferred over Euclidean distance for text embeddings. Answers to check yourself: Q1: a.b = 2 - 4 + 0 = -2; ||a|| = sqrt14. Q2: sqrt(9+16+144) = 13. Q4: \\(\\begin{pmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\), result (-3, 2). Q6: det = 10, inverse = (1/10)\\(\\begin{pmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}\\). Q7: dependent (second = 2 x first). Q10: proj = (2.5, 2.5).", "a": "<p>Answer not given in the notes file.</p>", "tag": "hard", "qHtml": "<p>Explain in 2-3 lines why cosine similarity is preferred over Euclidean distance for text embeddings.</p>\n<p><em>Answers to check yourself: Q1: a.b = 2 - 4 + 0 = -2; ||a|| = sqrt14. Q2: sqrt(9+16+144) = 13. Q4: \\(\\begin{pmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{pmatrix}\\), result (-3, 2). Q6: det = 10, inverse = (1/10)\\(\\begin{pmatrix}6 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{pmatrix}\\). Q7: dependent (second = 2 x first). Q10: proj = (2.5, 2.5).</em></p>"}], "file": "Semester_2/MTAI-MathematicsForAI/MTAI-M01.md"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Eigenvalues, SVD & PCA", "tagline": "Find the directions a matrix cannot rotate, then use them to compress data without losing much.", "minutes": 15, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>A matrix moves space around. Most arrows get stretched and turned; a few come out pointing the same way, only longer or shorter. Those are the <strong>eigenvectors</strong>, and the stretch factors are the <strong>eigenvalues</strong>.</p>\n<p>That one fact unlocks three things: diagonalisation makes repeated application easy, SVD extends it to rectangular matrices, and PCA asks which directions your data varies in.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why it matters</span><p>Know the stable directions of a matrix and you understand it after one step and after a thousand. Networks, Markov chains and compression all need that.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>You need determinants, matrix products and the identity I. New symbols:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>λ</strong> (lambda) is an eigenvalue, <strong>v</strong> its eigenvector.</li>\n<li><strong>det(A − λI) = 0</strong> is the characteristic equation for λ.</li>\n<li><strong>diag(a,b)</strong> has a, b on the diagonal and zeros elsewhere.</li>\n<li><strong>σ</strong> is a singular value, always ≥ 0; <strong>Σ</strong> in SVD holds them on its diagonal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two warnings: eigenvalues can be complex, and eigenvectors are fixed only up to scale — (1,1) and (7,7) are the same one.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.1", "title": "Eigenvalues and eigenvectors (syllabus 2.1)", "html": "<p>An eigenvector v of a square matrix A is a non-zero vector that A does not turn, only rescales: A v = λ v. \"Eigen\" is German for \"own\" — these are the matrix's private directions.</p>\n<p>Rewrite as (A − λI)v = 0. A non-zero v exists only if that matrix is singular, so det(A − λI) = 0, a polynomial in λ of degree n.</p>\n<p>Worked 2×2: A = [[2,1],[1,2]] gives (2−λ)² − 1 = (λ−3)(λ−1), so λ = 3 or 1. For λ = 3, y = x gives v = (1,1); for λ = 1, x + y = 0 gives v = (1,−1). Check: A(1,1) = (3,3) ✓.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>Eigenvector = stable direction of a transformation. In a deep network the directions surviving many layers are top eigenvectors of the weight products; a Markov chain settles into the eigenvector for λ = 1.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.2", "title": "Diagonalisation, symmetry, quadratic forms (syllabus 2.2–2.3)", "html": "<p>With independent eigenvectors as the columns of P and eigenvalues on the diagonal of D, A = P D P⁻¹, so powers become trivial: A² = P D² P⁻¹.</p>\n<p>Example: A = [[4,1],[2,3]] has λ = 5, 2 with v = (1,1), (1,−2), so D = diag(5,2) and A² has eigenvalues 25, 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Symmetric</strong> matrices (A = Aᵀ) are the well-behaved case: their eigenvalues are real and their eigenvectors orthogonal, so P can be orthonormal with P⁻¹ = Pᵀ. Covariance matrices and Hessians are symmetric, which is why ML feels clean.</p>\n<p>A <strong>quadratic form</strong> is xᵀA x, which for 2×2 looks like ax₁² + 2bx₁x₂ + cx₂². A is <strong>positive definite</strong> when xᵀA x &gt; 0 for all non-zero x — a bowl. Test: all eigenvalues positive. [[2,1],[1,2]] gives 3 and 1, so it is; [[2,4],[4,2]] gives 6 and −2, a saddle.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.3", "title": "Singular Value Decomposition (syllabus 2.4, 2.8)", "html": "<p>Eigenvectors need a square matrix. SVD works for any m×n matrix: A = U Σ Vᵀ, with U and V having orthonormal columns and Σ holding the non-negative <strong>singular values</strong> σ₁ ≥ σ₂ ≥ … on its diagonal.</p>\n<p>Read it as three moves: Vᵀ rotates the input into a good frame, Σ stretches each axis by σᵢ, U rotates into the output space. Every matrix is rotate, stretch, rotate.</p>\n<p>The link: σᵢ = √λᵢ of AᵀA, and the columns of V are its eigenvectors. For A = [[3,0],[0,−2]], AᵀA = diag(9,4), so σ = 3, 2 with V = I and U = [[1,0],[0,−1]]. The minus sign lives in U, since singular values are never negative.</p>\n<p>Keeping the top k singular values gives the best possible rank-k approximation of A: compression, denoising and latent-factor models in one sentence.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--bonus\"><span class=\"tag\">Beyond syllabus</span><p>Nobody computes SVD by hand: <code>np.linalg.svd</code> uses iterative methods at roughly O(mn²).</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.4", "title": "PCA and dimensionality reduction (syllabus 2.5–2.6)", "html": "<p><strong>Variance</strong> is the spread of one feature; <strong>covariance</strong> says whether two move together, positive meaning they rise together. Collect all pairs into C, which is symmetric, so 2.2 applies.</p>\n<p>PCA in three steps: centre each column, form C = (1/(n−1))·XᵀX, then take the eigenvectors of C sorted by eigenvalue. The top one is the direction of greatest variance, <strong>PC1</strong>, and symmetry makes the components orthogonal.</p>\n<p>Numbers: (1,1), (2,3), (3,2), (4,4) have mean (2.5, 2.5). Centred, C = [[1.667,1.333],[1.333,1.667]] with eigenvalues 3.0 and 0.333, eigenvectors (1,1)/√2 and (1,−1)/√2. PC1 holds 90% of the variance.</p>\n<p>Keep the top k components and you store k numbers per point instead of d. You keep big spreads and drop small ones (usually noise) — but a distinction living only in a dropped direction is gone.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.5", "title": "Factorisation, conditioning and the AI view (syllabus 2.7, 2.9–2.10)", "html": "<p><strong>Matrix factorisation</strong> writes a big matrix as a product of thin ones. A ratings matrix with 10⁵ users and 10⁴ films is mostly empty, but if taste is driven by ~50 hidden factors it is nearly rank 50; fitting A ≈ U Vᵀ recovers them and predicts missing entries. Word embeddings and LoRA fine-tuning have the same shape.</p>\n<p><strong>Norms</strong> measure size: Frobenius is √(Σ aᵢⱼ²), and the spectral norm is σ₁, the most any unit vector can be stretched.</p>\n<p><strong>Conditioning</strong> is κ(A) = σ<sub>max</sub>/σ<sub>min</sub>. Large κ means tiny input changes cause huge output changes, so solving loses accuracy and descent zig-zags — the honest reason we prefer SVD and QR to raw inverses.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>PCA, embeddings, recommenders and image compression say one thing: real data is approximately low rank. Finding the few directions that matter is what \"structure\" means.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[Av = \\lambda v, \\;\\; v \\ne 0; \\qquad \\det(A - \\lambda I) = 0\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> v is a direction A cannot rotate; λ is its stretch. The determinant equation finds λ.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Stability, repeated application, long-run behaviour.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> [[2,1],[1,2]]: λ² − 4λ + 3 = 0 gives λ = 3, 1.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[A = PDP^{-1}, \\qquad A^{k} = PD^{k}P^{-1}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Switch to the eigenvector basis, act diagonally, switch back.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Matrix powers, difference equations, repeated steps.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> [[4,1],[2,3]] has D = diag(5,2), so A² has eigenvalues 25, 4.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[x^{\\mathsf T}Ax > 0 \\ \\text{ for all } x \\ne 0 \\qquad \\iff \\qquad \\text{all } \\lambda_i > 0\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Positive definite: the quadratic form is a bowl, never a saddle.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Hessian tests, convexity, minimum checks.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> [[2,0],[0,5]] gives 2x₁² + 5x₂², always positive.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[A = U\\Sigma V^{\\mathsf T}, \\qquad \\sigma_i = \\sqrt{\\lambda_i(A^{\\mathsf T}A)}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Any matrix is rotate, stretch by the σ's, rotate.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Rectangular matrices, compression, rank-k approximation.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> [[3,0],[0,−2]] = [[1,0],[0,−1]]·diag(3,2)·I.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[C = \\frac{1}{n-1}X^{\\mathsf T}X \\ \\text{ (centred)}, \\qquad \\kappa(A) = \\frac{\\sigma_{\\max}}{\\sigma_{\\min}}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> C holds variances and covariances; κ measures numerical sensitivity.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> PCA; judging whether a solve will be accurate.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> κ(diag(3,2)) = 1.5 is fine; κ(diag(100,0.01)) = 10000 is not.</div>\n</div></div>\n<h4>Which formula when</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>I need to...</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Find preserved directions</td><td>det(A − λI) = 0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Apply a matrix many times</td><td>Aᵏ = P Dᵏ P⁻¹</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Check a minimum</td><td>Hessian eigenvalues all positive</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Factor a rectangular matrix</td><td>A = U Σ Vᵀ</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Find directions of variance</td><td>eigenvectors of the covariance matrix</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Judge numerical fragility</td><td>κ = σ<sub>max</sub>/σ<sub>min</sub></td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — eigen-decomposition of a 2×2</h4>\n<p>A = [[4,1],[2,3]]: det(A − λI) = λ² − 7λ + 10 = (λ−5)(λ−2), so λ = 5, 2.</p>\n<p>For λ = 5, −x + y = 0 gives (1,1); for λ = 2, 2x + y = 0 gives (1,−2). Check A(1,−2) = (2,−4) ✓.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">λ = 5 with (1,1); λ = 2 with (1,−2)</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — PCA by hand</h4>\n<p>Points (1,1), (2,3), (3,2), (4,4), mean (2.5,2.5). Centred, Σx² = Σy² = 5 and Σxy = 4, so C = [[1.667,1.333],[1.333,1.667]].</p>\n<p>Eigenvalues 3.0 and 0.333, so PC1 = (1,1)/√2 carries 90% of the spread.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">PC1 = (1,1)/√2 with 90% of variance</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Forgetting to centre the columns before PCA. Without centring you analyse distance from the origin, not spread, and PC1 often just points at the mean.</p></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Eigenvalues of A + B are not λ(A) + λ(B).</li>\n<li>Only symmetric matrices guarantee real eigenvalues; rotations give complex pairs.</li>\n<li>A singular value is never negative — the sign belongs to U or V.</li>\n<li>Eigenvectors are not unique: any non-zero multiple is correct.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>\"Find the eigenvalues, eigenvectors and diagonalise\" is 5–6 marks: characteristic equation, roots, one vector per root, then P and D in matching order.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>A v = λ v: v is a direction A cannot rotate, λ its stretch factor.</li>\n<li>Solve det(A − λI) = 0 for λ, then (A − λI)v = 0 for v.</li>\n<li>A = P D P⁻¹ makes powers easy: Aᵏ = P Dᵏ P⁻¹.</li>\n<li>Symmetric: real eigenvalues, orthogonal eigenvectors, P⁻¹ = Pᵀ.</li>\n<li>Positive definite ⇔ all eigenvalues positive ⇔ bowl ⇔ minimum.</li>\n<li>SVD works for any shape; truncation gives the best low-rank fit.</li>\n<li>PCA = centre, covariance, top eigenvectors; eigenvalue = variance kept.</li>\n<li>κ = σ<sub>max</sub>/σ<sub>min</sub>; large κ means fragile numerics.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A = [[4, 1], [2, 3]].", "a": "<p>det(A − λI) = λ² − 7λ + 10 = (λ−5)(λ−2), so λ = 5, 2.</p>\n<p>λ = 5: −x + y = 0 gives (1,1). λ = 2: 2x + y = 0 gives (1,−2). Check A(1,1) = (5,5) ✓.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "For A = [[2,1],[1,2]], find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors and show they are orthogonal.", "a": "<p>det = (2−λ)² − 1 = (λ−3)(λ−1), so λ = 3, 1 with eigenvectors (1,1) and (1,−1).</p>\n<p>Dot product 1 − 1 = 0, so they are orthogonal — guaranteed because A is symmetric.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Diagonalise A = [[4,1],[2,3]] and use D to get the eigenvalues of A².", "a": "<p>P = [[1,1],[1,−2]], D = diag(5,2), so A = P D P⁻¹ and A² = P D² P⁻¹ with D² = diag(25,4).</p>\n<p>Check: A² = [[18,7],[14,11]], trace 29 = 25 + 4, determinant 100 = 25·4 ✓.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Write the quadratic form of A = [[2,0],[0,5]] and decide positive definiteness. Repeat for B = [[1,3],[3,1]].", "a": "<p>For A, q = 2x₁² + 5x₂² ≥ 0, zero only at the origin: positive definite (eigenvalues 2, 5).</p>\n<p>For B, (1−λ)² − 9 = 0 gives λ = 4, −2: indefinite, a saddle. Along (1,−1), q = 1 − 6 + 1 = −4 &lt; 0.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Compute the SVD of A = [[3,0],[0,−2]] and say where the minus sign ends up.", "a": "<p>AᵀA = diag(9,4), so σ = 3, 2, Σ = diag(3,2) and V = I.</p>\n<p>uᵢ = A vᵢ/σᵢ gives u₁ = (1,0) and u₂ = (0,−1), so U = [[1,0],[0,−1]].</p>\n<p>A = U Σ Vᵀ rebuilds [[3,0],[0,−2]] ✓; the minus lives in U because σ is non-negative.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "Four points (1,1), (2,3), (3,2), (4,4): run PCA, give PC1 and the variance it captures, and say what you lose keeping one dimension.", "a": "<p>Means 2.5 each; centred, Σx² = Σy² = 5, Σxy = 4, so C = [[1.667,1.333],[1.333,1.667]].</p>\n<p>Eigenvalues 3.0, 0.333; PC1 = (1,1)/√2 captures 3.0/3.333 = 90%.</p>\n<p>Projecting merges (2,3) and (3,2): the 10% of spread along (1,−1) is lost.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/MTAI-MathematicsForAI/MTAI-M02.md"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Multivariable Calculus", "tagline": "Measure how fast the error moves when a weight changes, then bundle all the nudges into one gradient.", "minutes": 14, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>The derivative answers one question: if I nudge the input a little, how much does the output move? In AI the input is usually a weight and the output is the error. With fifty thousand weights you need every nudge at once, and that object is the <strong>gradient</strong>.</p>\n<p>So we build up in order: slope for one variable, slope per direction for many, the gradient vector, then the chain rule that turns a stack of functions into one sensitivity number.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why it matters</span><p>Backpropagation is not a deep-learning invention. It is the chain rule on a long composition, with intermediates stored so nothing is computed twice.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>You need school differentiation: powers, sums, tangent lines. New symbols, in words:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Δx</strong> is a small change in x, so Δy/Δx is an average rate of change.</li>\n<li><strong>f′(x)</strong>, also dy/dx, is the derivative: the instantaneous rate.</li>\n<li><strong>∂f/∂x</strong> is the partial derivative — differentiate in x, hold the rest still.</li>\n<li><strong>∇f</strong> (\"grad f\") is the gradient, the vector of all partials.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When a symbol has a subscript, ask what it indexes. Most confusion here is losing track of what is held constant.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.1", "title": "Limits, continuity, the derivative and its rules (syllabus 3.1–3.2)", "html": "<p>A <strong>limit</strong> asks where f(x) heads as x approaches a value, not what happens at it. f is <strong>continuous</strong> at a when that limit equals f(a) — no jump, no hole. The <strong>derivative</strong> is the limit of the average slope, f′(x) = lim (f(x+h) − f(x))/h as h → 0: slope of the tangent, and rate of change per unit input.</p>\n<p>Rules you use: power (d/dx xⁿ = n·xⁿ⁻¹), constant multiple, sum, product, quotient, and the chain rule d/dx f(g(x)) = f′(g(x))·g′(x).</p>\n<p>Example: y = (3x + 1)². Outer square, inner 3x + 1, so dy/dx = 2(3x + 1)·3 = 18x + 6; at x = 1 that is 24. Expanding first gives 9x² + 6x + 1 with the same derivative.</p>\n<p>Read the chain rule as multiplying local sensitivities: the inner changes 3 units per unit x, the outer 2·(inner) per unit inner, so the total is the product. That sentence is backpropagation in disguise.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.2", "title": "Functions of several variables, level sets, partials (syllabus 3.3–3.4)", "html": "<p>A function of two variables f(x, y) is a surface: a height above each point of the floor. A <strong>level set</strong> is where the height is constant — the contour lines of a trekking map. For f = x² + y², the level set at height 4 is the circle x² + y² = 4.</p>\n<p>In ML the loss L(w₁, …, w<sub>d</sub>) is a surface over weight space. You cannot draw it beyond three dimensions, but \"which way is downhill\" still makes sense.</p>\n<p>A <strong>partial derivative</strong> differentiates in one variable while treating the rest as constants. For f = x²y + 3y²: ∂f/∂x = 2xy and ∂f/∂y = x² + 6y. At (1, 2) those are 4 and 13.</p>\n<p>Meaning: standing at (1,2), a small step in x raises the height about 4 times as fast as the step size while y is frozen. Two numbers, two different walks.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Treating ∂f/∂x and df/dx as the same symbol. When variables depend on each other, the total derivative adds the indirect routes; the partial ignores them.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.3", "title": "The gradient vector (syllabus 3.5)", "html": "<p>Pack the partials into one vector: ∇f = (∂f/∂x, ∂f/∂y). For f = x²y + 3y² at (1,2), ∇f = (4, 13). One arrow now carries all the sensitivity.</p>\n<p>Two facts make it the star of training. It points in the direction of <strong>steepest ascent</strong> — walk along it and f climbs fastest — and its length ‖∇f‖ is that rate. So downhill is −∇f, which is gradient descent in one line.</p>\n<p>Why steepest ascent? A unit step in direction u changes f at rate ∇f·u, and a dot product is largest when the vectors align. The geometry of M1 does the work.</p>\n<p>Feel the numbers: at (1,2) the fastest climb is along (4,13) with length √185 ≈ 13.6, so a step of 0.01 that way raises f by about 0.136.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>Gradient = direction of steepest ascent. Every framework's <code>.backward()</code> fills a <code>.grad</code> tensor with this vector, and the optimizer moves weights by −η times it.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.4", "title": "Multivariable chain rule, Jacobian, Hessian (syllabus 3.6–3.7)", "html": "<p>When f depends on u and v and both depend on x, every route contributes. The chain rule adds them: df/dx = (∂f/∂u)(du/dx) + (∂f/∂v)(dv/dx). Each path gives a product of local slopes; the total is their sum.</p>\n<p>Example: f = u² + v with u = 2x and v = x². Then df/dx = 2u·2 + 1·2x = 8x + 2x = 10x. Check: f = 5x², derivative 10x ✓.</p>\n<p>This is the heart of backpropagation: the gradient for an early weight is the product of local derivatives along the path to the loss, summed over paths.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Jacobian</strong> collects all first derivatives of a vector-valued function, Jᵢⱼ = ∂fᵢ/∂xⱼ — the local linear map. The <strong>Hessian</strong> collects second derivatives, Hᵢⱼ = ∂²f/∂xᵢ∂xⱼ, is symmetric for smooth f, and gives curvature: positive definite means a bowl, mixed signs a saddle.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.5", "title": "Taylor approximation, integration, the AI reading (syllabus 3.8–3.10)", "html": "<p>Taylor says a smooth function looks like a polynomial near a point. First order: f(x+h) ≈ f(x) + f′(x)h, the tangent. Second order adds ½f″(x)h², matching the bend.</p>\n<p>Example: √9.2 with f = √x at x = 9, h = 0.2. f(9) = 3, f′(9) = 1/6 ≈ 0.1667, f″(9) = −1/108 ≈ −0.00926, so 3 + 0.03333 − 0.00019 = 3.03315. The true value is 3.033150: two terms give five correct decimals.</p>\n<p>In many variables the linear term is ∇f·h and the quadratic is ½hᵀHh; minimising that model gives the Newton step h = −H⁻¹∇f.</p>\n<p>The <strong>integral</strong> is accumulated area, the continuous Σ: a density gives probabilities as areas, P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = ∫ p dx, and E[X] = ∫ x·p(x) dx.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>The loss L(θ) is a surface over parameters. The gradient says which way to move, the Hessian whether you sit in a valley or on a saddle, and backprop is the chain rule on whatever produced L.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\frac{d}{dx}f(g(x)) = f'(g(x))\\cdot g'(x)\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Multiply local sensitivities along the chain.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Nested functions; conceptually every backward pass.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> (3x+1)² gives 2(3x+1)·3 = 18x + 6.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\nabla f = \\left( \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1}, \\ \\ldots, \\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_d} \\right)\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Vector of partials; it points uphill and its length is the steepest rate.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Choosing a search direction; gradient descent.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> x²y + 3y² at (1,2): (4, 13), length ≈ 13.6.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\frac{df}{dx} = \\sum_i \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial u_i}\\,\\frac{du_i}{dx}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Sum the products over every path from x to f.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Chained computations; the maths behind backprop.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> u² + v with u = 2x, v = x² gives 8x + 2x = 10x.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial f_i}{\\partial x_j}, \\qquad H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_i\\,\\partial x_j}, \\qquad f(x+h) \\approx f + f'h + \\tfrac{1}{2}f''h^2\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Jacobian = local linear map; Hessian = curvature; Taylor = local polynomial.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Second-order methods, convexity tests, quick approximations.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> √9.2 ≈ 3.03315 against a true 3.033150.</div>\n</div></div>\n<h4>Which formula when</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>I need to...</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Slope in one direction</td><td>∂f/∂x, others frozen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Direction of fastest increase</td><td>∇f</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Direction to reduce a loss</td><td>−∇f</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sensitivity through a chain</td><td>chain rule, sum over paths</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bowl or saddle?</td><td>Hessian eigenvalues</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — chain rule through a tiny computation</h4>\n<p>L = (wx + b − y)² with w = 2, x = 3, b = 1, y = 8.</p>\n<p>Forward: wx = 6, plus b is 7, minus y is −1, so L = 1. Local slopes: dL/d(residual) = −2, d(residual)/dw = x = 3.</p>\n<p>Multiply along the path: dL/dw = −2·3 = −6 and dL/db = −2. Check: L = (3w − 7)², so dL/dw = 6(3w − 7) = −6 ✓.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">dL/dw = −6, dL/db = −2</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — gradient and Hessian of a loss</h4>\n<p>L = w₁² + 3w₂² − 4w₁ + 6w₂ + 10.</p>\n<p>∇L = (2w₁ − 4, 6w₂ + 6) = 0 gives (2, −1). H = diag(2, 6) is positive definite, so it is a minimum, and L = 4 + 3 − 8 − 6 + 10 = 3.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">Minimum at (2, −1) with L = 3</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Sign confusion on the gradient. ∇f points uphill; to decrease the loss you move along −∇f. A lost minus sign inverts the whole training loop.</p></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Forgetting the inner derivative: d/dx (3x+1)² is 18x + 6, not 2(3x+1).</li>\n<li>A zero gradient proves a critical point, not a minimum — the Hessian decides.</li>\n<li>A pdf value can exceed 1; only its integral must be 1.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>\"Find the gradient at a point\" is 2 marks; \"find the Hessian and classify the critical point\" is 5–6. State the eigenvalue test explicitly.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Derivative = local sensitivity; the chain rule multiplies local slopes.</li>\n<li>Partial derivative: one variable at a time. Total derivative adds indirect routes.</li>\n<li>∇f points uphill, −∇f downhill, and ‖∇f‖ is the steepest rate.</li>\n<li>Multivariable chain rule = sum of products over all paths = backpropagation.</li>\n<li>Jacobian = first derivatives; Hessian = second derivatives and curvature.</li>\n<li>Taylor: f + f′h + ½f″h²; in many variables ∇f·h + ½hᵀHh.</li>\n<li>Integral = accumulated area; densities integrate to 1, E[X] = ∫ x p(x) dx.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Differentiate f(x) = (3x² + 1)⁴ and evaluate f′(1).", "a": "<p>Outer fourth power, inner derivative 6x: f′ = 4(3x²+1)³·6x = 24x(3x²+1)³.</p>\n<p>At x = 1: 24·64 = 1536. Sanity check: f(1) = 256, so a 1% step adds about 15.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "For f(x, y) = x²y + 3y², find both partials and the gradient at (1, 2).", "a": "<p>∂f/∂x = 2xy (y frozen); ∂f/∂y = x² + 6y (x frozen).</p>\n<p>At (1,2): 4 and 13, so ∇f = (4, 13). A step in y is 13 times as effective as its size.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Run three gradient-descent steps on L(w) = (w − 3)² from w = 0 with η = 0.1, and describe the pattern.", "a": "<p>L′ = 2(w−3). Steps: 0 → 0.6 (L′ = −6) → 1.08 (L′ = −4.8) → 1.464 (L′ = −3.84).</p>\n<p>The gap to 3 goes 3, 2.4, 1.92, 1.536: each step keeps 80%, since the gap is multiplied by (1 − 2η) = 0.8.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Apply the multivariable chain rule to f = u² + v with u = 2x, v = x², and verify by substitution.", "a": "<p>∂f/∂u = 2u, ∂f/∂v = 1, du/dx = 2, dv/dx = 2x, so df/dx = 4u + 2x = 8x + 2x = 10x.</p>\n<p>Direct: f = 4x² + x² = 5x², derivative 10x ✓. Drop a branch and you get 8x — the shape of a real backprop bug.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Find the Hessian of f(x, y) = x² + 4xy + 5y², classify its critical point, and say whether f is convex.", "a": "<p>∂f/∂x = 2x + 4y, ∂f/∂y = 4x + 10y. Both zero gives x = −2y and 2y = 0, so (0,0) is the only critical point.</p>\n<p>H = [[2,4],[4,10]]: det = 4 &gt; 0 with top-left 2 &gt; 0, so positive definite (eigenvalues ≈ 11.66, 0.34).</p>\n<p>H is constant and positive definite, so f is strictly convex and (0,0) is the unique global minimum.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "Approximate √9.2 by a second-order Taylor expansion of √x at x = 9 and compare with 3.033150.", "a": "<p>f(9) = 3, f′(9) = 1/6 ≈ 0.166667, f″(9) = −1/108 ≈ −0.009259.</p>\n<p>With h = 0.2: 3 + 0.033333 − 0.000185 = 3.033148, error about 2×10⁻⁶.</p>\n<p>The first-order answer alone is 3.033333, off by 0.00018 — the curvature term bought a hundredfold accuracy.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/MTAI-MathematicsForAI/MTAI-M03.md"}, {"n": 4, "title": "Optimization", "tagline": "Training a model is one sentence: find the parameters that make the loss smallest. Here is how.", "minutes": 14, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>\"Training a model\" sounds mysterious. It is not. You pick a model shape, pick a number that scores how wrong a parameter choice is, and search for the parameters that make that number smallest. That search is optimization, and it is the core of learning.</p>\n<p>Three tools do the work: the <strong>gradient</strong> says which way is downhill, the <strong>Hessian</strong> says what shape the ground is, and <strong>convexity</strong> says whether downhill leads to the bottom or to a trap.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why this is the core</span><p>A line, a logistic classifier and a hundred-layer network train by the same loop: measure the error, get the gradient, take a step, repeat.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>You need the gradient and Hessian from M3 and positive definiteness from M2. New symbols:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>min L(w)</strong> is the smallest value; <strong>argmin L(w)</strong> is the w achieving it.</li>\n<li><strong>w</strong> is the parameter vector; <strong>t</strong> counts iterations.</li>\n<li><strong>η</strong> (eta) is the learning rate, a small positive number you choose.</li>\n<li><strong>convex</strong> means bowl-shaped: the segment between two graph points lies above the graph.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The data is fixed during training, so the loss is a function of the parameters alone: \"learning from data\" is really \"searching a surface\".</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.1", "title": "Optimization as the core of learning (syllabus 4.1)", "html": "<p>Supervised learning has three ingredients: a model family (say a line), a loss scoring a candidate parameter set (say squared error), and a search over parameters. Only the third is optimization, but the first two decide how hard it is.</p>\n<p>Concrete: fit y = w·x to (1,2), (2,3), (3,5). Then L(w) = (w − 2)² + (2w − 3)² + (3w − 5)² = 14w² − 46w + 38, one number per candidate w.</p>\n<p>Different problems give different surfaces. Least squares is a smooth convex bowl with a closed-form answer; a neural network is smooth but wildly non-convex in millions of dimensions. The tools differ, the framing never does.</p>\n<p>Useful habit: before touching an algorithm, write down what is minimised, with respect to what, subject to what.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.2", "title": "Critical points, curvature and convexity (syllabus 4.2–4.3)", "html": "<p>At an interior minimum the surface cannot slope in any direction, so ∇L = 0. Those are <strong>critical points</strong>: candidates for minimum, maximum or saddle.</p>\n<p>The Hessian decides. Positive definite: minimum. Negative definite: maximum. Mixed signs: saddle. A zero eigenvalue means the test is silent.</p>\n<p>One variable: L = w² − 6w + 5 gives w = 3, and L″ = 2 &gt; 0, so it is a minimum with L = −4. For 2×2: det &gt; 0 with top-left &gt; 0 is a minimum, det &lt; 0 a saddle.</p>\n<p><strong>Convexity</strong> is stronger: f(θx + (1−θ)y) ≤ θf(x) + (1−θ)f(y) for θ in [0,1]. Then every local minimum is global and there are no saddle traps. Test with f″ ≥ 0, or a positive semi-definite Hessian everywhere. Sums of convex functions stay convex, which is why squared error plus an L2 penalty is safe.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.3", "title": "Gradient descent, SGD and momentum (syllabus 4.4–4.5)", "html": "<p>The algorithm is one line: w ← w − η∇L(w). The gradient points uphill, so the minus walks downhill; η sets the step size.</p>\n<p>By hand on L = (w − 3)², from w = 0 with η = 0.1: L′ = −6, so w₁ = 0.6; then L′ = −4.8 gives 1.08; then 1.464. It creeps toward 3, closing 20% of the gap per step.</p>\n<p>Learning-rate intuition with L = w², where the update is w ← w(1 − 2η). At η = 0.1 each step keeps 80% of w. At η = 1 the factor is −1, so w flips sign forever. At η = 1.5 the factor is −2 and the value explodes.</p>\n<p><strong>SGD</strong> uses one example or a mini-batch instead of the full dataset: noisier steps, far cheaper, faster progress. <strong>Momentum</strong> keeps a velocity, v ← βv − η∇L then w ← w + v with β ≈ 0.9, so consistent directions build speed while zig-zags cancel.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.4", "title": "Least squares, constraints and regularisation (syllabus 4.6–4.8)", "html": "<p><strong>Least squares as optimization:</strong> minimise L(w) = ‖Xw − y‖². The gradient is 2XᵀXw − 2Xᵀy, so setting it to zero gives XᵀXw = Xᵀy, the normal equations of M1. Geometry and calculus reach the same equation.</p>\n<p>Example: points (1,2), (2,3), (3,5) with y = c + d·t give XᵀX = [[3,6],[6,14]] and Xᵀy = (10,23), so d = 1.5 and c = 1/3.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints:</strong> to minimise f subject to g(x) = 0, the gradients are parallel at the optimum: ∇f = λ∇g. Minimising x² + y² subject to x + y = 4 gives 2x = λ = 2y, so x = y = 2 and f = 8.</p>\n<p><strong>Regularisation</strong> penalises size. L2 adds λ‖w‖² and shrinks weights smoothly; L1 adds λ‖w‖₁ and pushes some exactly to zero, so it selects features. Both are ordinary optimization with a modified objective.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.5", "title": "What goes wrong, and why this is the AI core (syllabus 4.9–4.10)", "html": "<p>Failures have signatures, and the loss curve is the first diagnostic. Too large a learning rate makes the loss jump or turn into NaN; a curve that falls then flattens may be a local minimum or simply out of steps; oscillation across a narrow valley means the step is too big for the curvature.</p>\n<p>Saddle points have zero gradient but are not minima, and they are far more common than bad local minima in high dimensions; SGD's noise helps escape them. Features on wildly different scales make the surface long and thin, so descent zig-zags.</p>\n<p>Checklist: standardise features, lower η by 10×, check the sign of the update, and watch training and validation loss separately.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>Gradient descent <b>is</b> how neural networks learn. This module is the mathematical core of training: everything else is choosing the loss and the step rule.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[w \\leftarrow w - \\eta\\,\\nabla L(w)\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Step opposite the gradient; η sets how far.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Almost every trained model; the default first attempt.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> L = (w−3)², w = 0, η = 0.1: w becomes 0.6.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\nabla L = 0, \\ \\ H \\text{ positive definite} \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\text{local minimum}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Zero slope finds candidates; curvature classifies them.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Small problems with a closed-form derivative.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> L = w² − 6w + 5: w = 3, L″ = 2 &gt; 0, L = −4.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[f(\\theta x + (1-\\theta)y) \\le \\theta f(x) + (1-\\theta)f(y)\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The chord lies above the graph — the bowl condition.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Proving a loss has no bad local minima.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> x² at x = 0, y = 2, θ = ½: 1 ≤ 2.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[X^{\\mathsf T}X\\,w = X^{\\mathsf T}y\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Normal equations: the least-squares optimum in one solve.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Linear regression with a modest feature count.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> (1,2),(2,3),(3,5): d = 1.5, c = 1/3.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\nabla f = \\lambda \\nabla g, \\qquad L_{\\text{reg}} = L + \\lambda\\|w\\|^2 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad L + \\lambda\\|w\\|_1\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Parallel gradients at a constrained optimum; a penalised loss.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Equality constraints; controlling overfitting.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> (w−5)² + w² is minimal at w = 2.5.</div>\n</div></div>\n<h4>Which formula when</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>I need to...</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Train anything with a gradient</td><td>w ← w − η∇L</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solve exactly, small problem</td><td>∇L = 0 plus Hessian check</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exact linear least squares</td><td>XᵀX w = Xᵀy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed up on big data</td><td>SGD or mini-batch</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Damp oscillation</td><td>momentum, β ≈ 0.9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Respect a constraint</td><td>∇f = λ∇g</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — gradient descent by hand</h4>\n<p>L(w) = (w − 3)², start w = 0, η = 0.1.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>w</th><th>L′(w)</th><th>L(w)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>−6</td><td>9</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>0.6</td><td>−4.8</td><td>5.76</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>1.08</td><td>−3.84</td><td>3.686</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>1.464</td><td>−3.072</td><td>2.359</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The gap to 3 shrinks by 0.8 each step, since the update multiplies (w − 3) by (1 − 2η).</p>\n<div class=\"res\">0 → 0.6 → 1.08 → 1.464, converging to 3</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — one problem, two routes</h4>\n<p>Fit y = c + d·t to (1,2), (2,3), (3,5). Normal equations: XᵀX = [[3,6],[6,14]], Xᵀy = (10,23).</p>\n<p>Doubling the first equation and subtracting gives 2d = 3, so d = 1.5 and c = 1/3. Gradient descent on the same loss arrives at the same point in many small steps.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">Both routes: y = 1/3 + 1.5t</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Sign and scale of the update. Writing w ← w + η∇L climbs instead of descends, and dropping the 1/n in a mean-squared loss multiplies your effective learning rate by n.</p></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Confusing min and argmin: \"smallest loss is 3\" and \"best w is (2,−1)\" are different answers.</li>\n<li>Zero gradient does not mean minimum; saddles are common in high dimensions.</li>\n<li>Regularise weights only, not the bias.</li>\n<li>L1 produces exact zeros, L2 does not. They are not interchangeable.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>\"Perform 2 iterations of gradient descent\" — show the gradient at every step, not just the final w. And \"derive the normal equations\": differentiate, set to zero, rearrange.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Training = minimising a loss over parameters. Say what, with respect to what, subject to what.</li>\n<li>∇L = 0 finds critical points; the Hessian classifies them.</li>\n<li>Convex: every local minimum is global. Test f″ ≥ 0 or a positive semi-definite Hessian.</li>\n<li>Gradient descent: w ← w − η∇L. Too small is slow, too large diverges.</li>\n<li>SGD uses mini-batches; momentum damps zig-zags.</li>\n<li>Least squares: ∇‖Xw − y‖² = 0 gives XᵀXw = Xᵀy.</li>\n<li>Constraints: ∇f = λ∇g. Regularisation: add λ‖w‖² or λ‖w‖₁.</li>\n<li>Read the loss curve first: rising means η too large, flat means stuck.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Find the critical point of f(w) = w² − 6w + 5 and classify it.", "a": "<p>f′ = 2w − 6 = 0 gives w = 3, and f″ = 2 &gt; 0 everywhere, so the function is convex and this is the global minimum.</p>\n<p>f(3) = −4. Report both: min f = −4 and argmin f = 3.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Starting at w = 4 with η = 0.25, do two gradient-descent steps on f(w) = w².", "a": "<p>f′ = 2w, so the update is w ← 0.5w.</p>\n<p>Step 1: 4 − 2 = 2. Step 2: 2 − 1 = 1. It halves each step because 2η = 0.5 &lt; 2.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Is f(x) = x⁴ convex? Is g(x) = x³ convex on R?", "a": "<p>f″ = 12x² ≥ 0 for all x, so f is convex.</p>\n<p>g″ = 6x changes sign, so g is concave for x &lt; 0 and convex for x &gt; 0 — not convex on R. At x = −2, y = 1, θ = ½: g(−0.5) = −0.125 while ½g(−2) + ½g(1) = −3.5, violating the inequality.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Fit y = c + d·t to (1,2), (2,3), (3,5) by the normal equations and check the residuals.", "a": "<p>Σ1 = 3, Σt = 6, Σt² = 14, so XᵀX = [[3,6],[6,14]]; Xᵀy = (10, 23).</p>\n<p>2d = 3 gives d = 1.5 and c = 1/3. Predictions 1.833, 3.333, 4.833; residuals 0.167, −0.333, 0.167, summing to 0.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Minimise x² + y² subject to x + y = 4 with a Lagrange multiplier, and confirm by substitution.", "a": "<p>∇f = (2x, 2y) and ∇g = (1,1), so 2x = λ = 2y, forcing x = y = 2 and f = 8, with λ = 4.</p>\n<p>By substitution y = 4 − x, so f = 2x² − 8x + 16; 4x − 8 = 0 gives x = 2 and f″ = 4 &gt; 0 ✓.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "A model minimises (w − 5)² + λw². Find the optimal w for λ = 0, 1 and 4, and explain what the penalty does.", "a": "<p>2(w − 5) + 2λw = 0 gives w = 5/(1+λ): 5, 2.5 and 1, tending to 0 as λ grows.</p>\n<p>The second derivative 2(1+λ) &gt; 0, so each is a minimum and the loss is convex for every λ ≥ 0.</p>\n<p>The penalty trades fit against weight size: slightly worse training error for a model that generalises.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/MTAI-MathematicsForAI/MTAI-M04.md"}, {"n": 5, "title": "Probability", "tagline": "Make a model say how sure it is, and update that belief correctly when evidence arrives.", "minutes": 14, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>A model that says \"spam\" is less useful than one that says \"87% chance of spam\". Probability is how a model expresses doubt, and how we score an answer that is inherently uncertain.</p>\n<p>We start with sample spaces and events, add conditioning (where intuition breaks), meet Bayes for updating belief, then random variables, expectation, the standard distributions, and the limit theorems that make sampling trustworthy.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">The one idea to keep</span><p>P(A | B) and P(B | A) are different numbers. Nearly every real probability mistake — tests, spam filters, fraud alerts — is somebody swapping those two.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>You need fractions and percentages. New symbols, in words:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ω</strong> (omega) is the sample space: all possible outcomes.</li>\n<li>An <strong>event</strong> is a subset of Ω; P(A) lies between 0 and 1.</li>\n<li><strong>A ∩ B</strong> means \"both\", <strong>A ∪ B</strong> \"at least one\", <strong>Aᶜ</strong> \"not A\".</li>\n<li><strong>P(A | B)</strong> is read \"probability of A given B\".</li>\n<li><strong>X</strong> is a random variable; <strong>E[X]</strong> its mean; <strong>~</strong> means \"is distributed as\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Rule zero: the probabilities of all outcomes in Ω add to exactly 1.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.1", "title": "Foundations, conditioning and independence (syllabus 5.1–5.2)", "html": "<p>An experiment has outcomes; the set of all of them is Ω. For one die, Ω = {1,…,6} and \"even\" is {2,4,6} with probability 3/6.</p>\n<p>The three axioms: P(A) ≥ 0; P(Ω) = 1; if A and B cannot both happen then P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B). Hence P(Aᶜ) = 1 − P(A).</p>\n<p><strong>Conditional probability</strong> shrinks the world to B: P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B). With the die, P(even | &gt;3) = (2/6)/(3/6) = 2/3. Two cards without replacement are both aces with probability (4/52)(3/51) ≈ 0.0045.</p>\n<p>A and B are <strong>independent</strong> when P(A | B) = P(A), equivalently P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B). The <strong>multiplication rule</strong> P(A ∩ B) = P(A | B)P(B) always holds; independence is where it factorises. The <strong>law of total probability</strong> splits by cases: P(A) = Σ P(A | Bᵢ)P(Bᵢ). With machine 1 at 60% of items and 2% defective, machine 2 at 40% and 5%, P(defective) = 0.012 + 0.020 = 0.032.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Independent is not mutually exclusive. Mutually exclusive means P(A ∩ B) = 0; independent means P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B). Both cannot hold for non-zero probabilities.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.2", "title": "Bayes' theorem and naive Bayes (syllabus 5.3)", "html": "<p>Bayes' theorem flips a conditional: P(A | B) = P(B | A)P(A)/P(B). It is not a new axiom — write P(A ∩ B) two ways and divide.</p>\n<p>Spam filter: P(spam) = 0.3, and \"free\" appears in 60% of spam and 5% of ordinary mail. Denominator first: P(\"free\") = 0.6(0.3) + 0.05(0.7) = 0.215. Then P(spam | \"free\") = 0.18/0.215 ≈ 0.837.</p>\n<p>One word lifts belief from 0.3 to 0.84 — but the base rate still rules: if only 1% of mail were spam, the same word gives 0.006/0.0555 ≈ 0.108.</p>\n<p><strong>Naive Bayes</strong> is the classifier version: assume features are independent given the class, multiply likelihoods, pick the larger posterior. The assumption is usually false and the classifier still works — a useful example of a wrong model earning its place.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>Bayes = the spam filter. The same arithmetic drives medical screening, fraud alerts and any system that revises belief after evidence.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.3", "title": "Random variables, expectation and variance (syllabus 5.4–5.5)", "html": "<p>A random variable attaches a number to each outcome. Discrete ones have a <strong>pmf</strong>, p(x) = P(X = x) with Σ p(x) = 1. Continuous ones have a <strong>pdf</strong> f(x), and probability comes from area: P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = ∫ f dx from a to b.</p>\n<p>For a continuous variable P(X = c) = 0 for any single c, and a pdf value is a density, not a probability — it may exceed 1. The <strong>cdf</strong> F(x) = P(X ≤ x) works for both kinds and runs from 0 to 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Expectation</strong> is the long-run average, E[X] = Σ x·p(x); it need not be attainable, since a die has E[X] = 3.5. <strong>Variance</strong> is the average squared distance from the mean, Var(X) = E[X²] − (E[X])²; standard deviation is its root.</p>\n<p>For the die: E[X²] = 91/6 ≈ 15.167, so Var = 15.167 − 12.25 = 2.917 and sd ≈ 1.708. Uniform on [0,4]: f = 1/4, P(1 ≤ X ≤ 3) = 0.5, E[X] = 2.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.4", "title": "Key distributions and the Gaussian (syllabus 5.6–5.7)", "html": "<p><strong>Bernoulli(p)</strong>: one yes/no trial, mean p, variance p(1−p). <strong>Binomial(n, p)</strong>: successes in n trials, mean np. <strong>Uniform</strong>: flat density. <strong>Gaussian N(μ, σ²)</strong>: the bell. <strong>Poisson(λ)</strong>: rare-event counts, mean = variance = λ.</p>\n<p>The Gaussian dominates because of the CLT, because sums of many small effects look normal, and because two numbers describe it fully. About 68% of its mass lies within one σ.</p>\n<p><strong>Standardisation</strong> z = (x − μ)/σ turns any Gaussian into N(0,1), so one table serves every problem: a mark of 75 with μ = 60 and σ = 10 is z = 1.5, past roughly 93% of the class.</p>\n<p>The same operation scales features: subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation before any distance or gradient method runs.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.5", "title": "Joint, marginal, conditional, LLN and CLT (syllabus 5.8–5.10)", "html": "<p>A <strong>joint</strong> gives P(X = x, Y = y) for each pair; a <strong>marginal</strong> sums over the variable you ignore; a <strong>conditional</strong> divides joint by marginal.</p>\n<p>Table: P(0,0) = 0.1, P(0,1) = 0.2, P(1,0) = 0.3, P(1,1) = 0.4. Marginals P(X=1) = 0.7 and P(Y=1) = 0.6. Conditional P(Y=1 | X=1) = 0.4/0.7 ≈ 0.571, which differs from 0.6, so the two are dependent.</p>\n<p><strong>Covariance</strong> is E[XY] − E[X]E[Y] = 0.4 − 0.42 = −0.02 here. <strong>Correlation</strong> divides by both standard deviations, giving a scale-free number in [−1,1]. Stack all feature covariances and you have the matrix C of M2 — PCA diagonalises exactly this.</p>\n<p><strong>LLN:</strong> the average of many independent samples approaches the true mean. <strong>CLT:</strong> that average is approximately normal with sd σ/√n — why averaging 100 samples cuts noise tenfold.</p>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[P(A \\mid B) = \\frac{P(A \\cap B)}{P(B)}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Probability of A inside the smaller world where B is true.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any \"given that\" question; the base of everything here.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> Die: P(even | &gt;3) = (2/6)/(3/6) = 2/3.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[P(A \\cap B) = P(A \\mid B)P(B); \\qquad \\text{independent} \\Rightarrow P(A)P(B)\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The multiplication rule, with independence as the special case.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> \"Both happen\" questions; sampling with or without replacement.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> Two aces: (4/52)(3/51) ≈ 0.0045.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[P(A) = \\sum_i P(A \\mid B_i)P(B_i); \\qquad P(A \\mid B) = \\frac{P(B \\mid A)P(A)}{P(B)}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Total probability builds the denominator; Bayes flips the conditional.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Mixed sources, tests, filters, diagnosis.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> 0.6(0.02) + 0.4(0.05) = 0.032; spam posterior 0.837.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[E[X] = \\sum_x x\\,p(x), \\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Centre of mass, and average squared spread about it.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Summarising a distribution; standardising features.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> Die: E = 3.5, E[X²] = 15.167, Var = 2.917.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[z = \\frac{x - \\mu}{\\sigma}, \\qquad \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y) = E[XY] - E[X]E[Y]\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Distance in standard deviations; whether two variables move together.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Standardisation, feature redundancy, the covariance matrix behind PCA.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> 75 with μ = 60, σ = 10 gives z = 1.5; table gives Cov = −0.02.</div>\n</div></div>\n<h4>Which formula when</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>I need to...</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Probability given extra information</td><td>P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Both events happen</td><td>multiplication rule</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Combine two sources</td><td>law of total probability</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Evidence to cause</td><td>Bayes' theorem</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Average outcome</td><td>E[X]</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spread around the average</td><td>Var = E[X²] − μ²</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Error of an average of n samples</td><td>σ/√n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — a medical test and the base rate</h4>\n<p>Disease affects 1%; the test catches 99% of cases and falsely flags 5% of healthy people.</p>\n<p>P(+) = 0.99(0.01) + 0.05(0.99) = 0.0099 + 0.0495 = 0.0594, so P(disease | +) = 0.0099/0.0594 ≈ 0.167.</p>\n<p>Roughly 1 in 6, not a paradox: the healthy group is 99 times larger, so its false alarms outnumber the true cases.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">P(disease | positive) ≈ 0.167</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — die: expectation, variance, conditioning</h4>\n<p>E[X] = 21/6 = 3.5 and E[X²] = 91/6 ≈ 15.167, so Var = 2.917 and sd ≈ 1.708.</p>\n<p>P(even ∩ ≥4) = P({4,6}) = 2/6 and P(≥4) = 3/6, so P(even | ≥4) = 2/3.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">E[X] = 3.5, Var = 2.917, P(even | ≥4) = 2/3</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Swapping P(A | B) with P(B | A). \"99% of patients with the disease test positive\" is not \"99% of positives have the disease\".</p></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Multiplying probabilities without checking independence; sampling without replacement breaks it at once.</li>\n<li>Treating a pdf value as a probability. Densities integrate to 1; they need not stay below 1.</li>\n<li>Forgetting the base rate — rare events stay rare after strong evidence.</li>\n<li>Zero covariance does not mean independence, only no linear relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>Bayes is 5–6 marks in stages: given conditionals, denominator by total probability, Bayes, then one line of interpretation.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B): condition on B and the denominator becomes P(B).</li>\n<li>Independent ⇔ P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B); not the same as mutually exclusive.</li>\n<li>Total probability splits by cases; Bayes flips a conditional.</li>\n<li>Discrete → pmf, Σp = 1. Continuous → pdf, area = probability.</li>\n<li>E[X] is the weighted mean; Var = E[X²] − (E[X])².</li>\n<li>Bernoulli, Binomial, Uniform, Gaussian, Poisson: know each parameter and mean.</li>\n<li>LLN: averages settle. CLT: averages are normal with sd σ/√n.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "A fair die is rolled. Find P(even), P(>4), P(even ∩ >4) and P(even | >4).", "a": "<p>P(even) = 0.5 and P(&gt;4) = 2/6 = 1/3, so P(even ∩ &gt;4) = P({6}) = 1/6.</p>\n<p>P(even | &gt;4) = (1/6)/(2/6) = 1/2, equal to the unconditional value, and indeed P(even)P(&gt;4) = 1/6: independent.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Machine 1 makes 60% of items with 2% defective; machine 2 makes 40% with 5% defective. A defective item — which machine?", "a": "<p>P(D) = 0.6(0.02) + 0.4(0.05) = 0.032, so P(M1 | D) = 0.012/0.032 = 0.375.</p>\n<p>Even though machine 1 makes most items, a defective one is more likely from machine 2: its rate is 2.5 times higher.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "A word appears in 60% of spam and 5% of normal mail; spam is 30% of mail. Find P(spam | word), then recompute for a 1% spam rate.", "a": "<p>P(W) = 0.6(0.3) + 0.05(0.7) = 0.215, so P(S | W) = 0.18/0.215 ≈ 0.837.</p>\n<p>With a 1% spam rate: P(W) = 0.006 + 0.0495 = 0.0555, so P(S | W) ≈ 0.108.</p>\n<p>Same word, 0.84 versus 0.11 — the evidence did not change, the prior did.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "X is uniform on [0, 4]. Give the pdf, the cdf, P(1 ≤ X ≤ 3) and E[X].", "a": "<p>f(x) = 1/4 on [0,4], zero elsewhere, since a constant over length 4 must integrate to 1. Then F(x) = x/4.</p>\n<p>P(1 ≤ X ≤ 3) = F(3) − F(1) = 0.5 and E[X] = ∫ x/4 dx = 2. Note P(X = 2) = 0 even though 2 is the mean.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "For the joint table P(0,0)=0.2, P(0,1)=0.1, P(1,0)=0.3, P(1,1)=0.4, find the marginals, P(Y=1|X=1) and the covariance. Are X and Y independent?", "a": "<p>Total is 1.0; marginals P(X=1) = 0.7 and P(Y=1) = 0.5.</p>\n<p>P(Y=1 | X=1) = 0.4/0.7 ≈ 0.571 ≠ 0.5, and P(1,1) = 0.4 ≠ 0.35, so dependent.</p>\n<p>E[XY] = 0.4 and E[X]E[Y] = 0.35, so Cov = 0.05; Corr = 0.05/(0.458 × 0.5) ≈ 0.218.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "Scores are Gaussian with mean 60 and sd 10. Standardise 75, then explain what the CLT says about the average of 100 scores.", "a": "<p>z = (75 − 60)/10 = 1.5, with roughly 7% scoring above it.</p>\n<p>CLT: the average of 100 scores is approximately normal with mean 60 and sd 10/√100 = 1, so a class average of 63 is three standard errors out — very unlikely, though 63 for one student is ordinary.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/MTAI-MathematicsForAI/MTAI-M05.md"}, {"n": 6, "title": "From Maths to Models", "tagline": "Wire the four strands into one pipeline, then read real ML code and papers without fear.", "minutes": 14, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Five moves turn data into a model, and you have met each one separately. Data becomes a matrix (M1). The model is a function of that matrix and some weights (M1, M3). The error becomes a loss (M3). Learning is minimising it (M4). The output can be read as a probability (M5).</p>\n<pre class=\"ascii\">data X (n x d) --model--&gt; y_hat = f(X; w)\n                              |\n                         loss L(w)\n                              |\n                w &lt;- w - eta * grad L(w)\n                              |\n                   trained w -&gt; predict + confidence</pre>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why this module exists</span><p>Separately these were exam topics. Together they are the reason a machine can learn from examples.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>Everything here is recap. The notation, in words:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>X</strong> is the data matrix, n rows (examples) by d columns (features); <strong>x</strong> is one row.</li>\n<li><strong>y</strong> holds true targets; <strong>ŷ</strong> (\"y-hat\") is the prediction.</li>\n<li><strong>w</strong> is the weight vector, <strong>b</strong> the bias; together they are θ.</li>\n<li><strong>L</strong> is the loss, <strong>∇L</strong> its gradient, <strong>η</strong> the learning rate.</li>\n<li><strong>σ</strong> here is the sigmoid; <strong>⊙</strong> means entry-by-entry multiply.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If any of these feels shaky, revisit the module named beside it — this one assumes all four strands.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.1", "title": "The data-to-model pipeline (syllabus 6.1)", "html": "<p>Step one, representation: put the data in a matrix X of shape n×d, one row per example. Step two, model: choose a family ŷ = f(X; w) — a line, a logistic curve, a stack of layers — where w are the unknowns.</p>\n<p>Step three, loss: choose a number scoring how wrong a w is, L(w) = (1/n)Σ ℓ(ŷᵢ, yᵢ). Step four, optimization: find the minimising w. Step five, use: predict on new rows and attach a probability where you can.</p>\n<p>Most practical work is choosing well rather than deriving: squared error suits predicting a number, cross-entropy suits predicting a probability.</p>\n<p>The pipeline also localises bugs. Wrong shapes: step one. Loss that never decreases: steps three or four. Great training score but poor test score: step two, the model is too flexible.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.2", "title": "Linear regression end-to-end, two routes (syllabus 6.2)", "html": "<p>Model ŷ = Xw + b, loss the squared error (1/n)‖Xw + b − y‖². One problem, two mathematical routes to the same answer.</p>\n<p><strong>Route A, linear algebra.</strong> Setting the gradient to zero gives the normal equations XᵀXw = Xᵀy, so w = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy: project y onto the column space of X. Exact, no tuning, roughly O(d³).</p>\n<p><strong>Route B, calculus and optimization.</strong> Compute ∇L = (2/n)Xᵀ(Xw − y) and iterate w ← w − η∇L. Slower and needs a learning rate, but it survives d in the millions, where forming XᵀX is hopeless.</p>\n<p>Numbers: (0,1), (1,3), (2,4). Route A gives d = 1.5, c = 7/6. Route B from c = d = 0 with η = 0.05: residuals −1, −3, −4, gradients −16 and −22, so c = 0.8, d = 1.1 after one step.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.3", "title": "Logistic regression (syllabus 6.3)", "html": "<p>For a yes/no answer a line is wrong: it predicts values like 3.7 and −2. Squash the score through the <strong>sigmoid</strong> σ(z) = 1/(1 + e<sup>−z</sup>), which maps any real number into (0,1). Then p = σ(w·x + b) is read as P(y = 1 | x).</p>\n<p>σ(0) = 0.5, σ(2) ≈ 0.881, σ(−2) ≈ 0.119: an S-curve, steep near zero and flat at the ends.</p>\n<p>Loss is cross-entropy, L = −[y log p + (1−y) log(1−p)]: truth 1 with p = 0.9 costs 0.105, with p = 0.1 it costs 2.303.</p>\n<p>Training is clean: the gradient is (p − y)·x for weights and (p − y) for the bias. One step on x = 1, y = 1, w = b = 0, η = 0.1: p = 0.5, gradient −0.5, so w = 0.05. There is no closed form, because the sigmoid makes the equations non-linear.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>Here all four strands meet: a matrix product, a derivative, a descent step, a probability as output. A neural network is this recipe with more layers.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.4", "title": "One neuron and a tiny network (syllabus 6.4)", "html": "<p>A layer is a matrix product plus a bias plus a non-linearity: h = ReLU(W₁x + b₁), then ŷ = W₂h + b₂. Without the non-linearity two layers collapse into one, since W₂(W₁x) = (W₂W₁)x.</p>\n<p>Take x = 1, w₁ = 2, b₁ = −1, w₂ = 3, b₂ = 0, y = 5, loss ½(ŷ − y)².</p>\n<p>Forward: z₁ = 1, h = ReLU(1) = 1, ŷ = 3, L = ½(3−5)² = 2.</p>\n<p>Backward: dL/dŷ = −2, so dL/dw₂ = −2 and dL/dh = −6. ReLU is active, so dL/dw₁ = −6·x = −6.</p>\n<p>With η = 0.1: w₂ = 3.2, w₁ = 2.6, giving h = 1.6, ŷ = 5.12, L ≈ 0.0072 — from 2 to 0.007 in one step.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--ai\"><span class=\"tag\">In AI</span><p>That is the skeleton of deep learning: forward pass as linear algebra, loss as a number, gradients by the chain rule, update by descent. Libraries differ only in scale.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.5", "title": "Why the pieces fit, notation and numerical reality (syllabus 6.5–6.8)", "html": "<p>Four jobs, four strands. <strong>Linear algebra</strong> represents: data as matrices, models as products. <strong>Calculus</strong> measures sensitivity. <strong>Optimization</strong> turns sensitivity into a step. <strong>Probability</strong> quantifies doubt.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>You see</th><th>It means</th><th>From</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>X ∈ R<sup>n×d</sup></td><td>n examples, d features</td><td>M1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>W₁ ∈ R<sup>h×d</sup></td><td>layer weights, h outputs from d inputs</td><td>M1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>∇<sub>θ</sub> L</td><td>gradient of the loss over all parameters</td><td>M3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>argmin<sub>θ</sub> L</td><td>the parameters with the smallest loss</td><td>M4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>E<sub>q</sub>[·]</td><td>average under distribution q</td><td>M5</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Libraries use stable factorisations and automatic differentiation, but the maths gives you diagnosis: a NaN loss means log of zero or a step too big; train loss falling while validation rises means overfitting; badly scaled features make XᵀX ill-conditioned.</p>\n<p>Next stop is <em>Mathematics for Data Science (T03)</em> — statistics and inference, where M5's probability turns into decisions.</p>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Formulas, rules and properties", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[\\hat{y} = Xw + b\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> A linear model: one dot product of features with weights per example.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Predicting a number; the last layer of many regressors.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> x = (2,1), w = (3,−1), b = 0.5 gives 5.5.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[X^{\\mathsf T}X\\,w = X^{\\mathsf T}y \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad w = (X^{\\mathsf T}X)^{-1}X^{\\mathsf T}y\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The exact least-squares solution, from projection or from ∇L = 0.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Moderate d, when you want one solve and no tuning.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> (0,1),(1,3),(2,4) gives c = 7/6, d = 1.5.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[p = \\sigma(w\\cdot x + b) = \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-(w\\cdot x + b)}}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Squash a score into a probability between 0 and 1.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Binary classification; the output unit of a two-class network.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> Score 0 gives 0.5; score 2 gives 0.881.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[L = -[\\, y\\log p + (1-y)\\log(1-p) \\,]\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Cross-entropy: heavy punishment for confident mistakes.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any model that outputs a probability.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> y = 1: p = 0.9 costs 0.105, p = 0.1 costs 2.303.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">\\[w \\leftarrow w - \\eta\\nabla L(w), \\qquad \\frac{dL}{dW_2} = \\delta_2 h^{\\mathsf T}\\]</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The universal training step, and the output-layer gradient.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Whenever no closed form exists — most of ML.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> Logistic, x = 1, y = 1, w = 0, η = 0.1: w becomes 0.05.</div>\n</div></div>\n<h4>Which formula when</h4>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>I need to...</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Predict a number</td><td>Xw + b with squared error</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Predict a class</td><td>sigmoid + cross-entropy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Exact regression answer</td><td>normal equations</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regression at large scale</td><td>gradient descent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gradients for a network</td><td>chain rule, layer by layer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diagnose a bad model</td><td>compare train and validation loss</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — linear regression, both routes</h4>\n<p>Points (0,1), (1,3), (2,4), model y = c + d·t.</p>\n<p>Route A: XᵀX = [[3,3],[3,5]], Xᵀy = (8,11), so d = 1.5 and c = 7/6 ≈ 1.167.</p>\n<p>Route B, one step from c = d = 0, η = 0.05: residuals −1, −3, −4, gradients −16 and −22, so c = 0.8 and d = 1.1.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">Both routes: y = 1.167 + 1.5t</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — one logistic step</h4>\n<p>x = 1, y = 1, w = b = 0, η = 0.1. Forward: z = 0, p = 0.5, loss = 0.693. Gradients −0.5 for both w and b.</p>\n<p>Update: w = b = 0.05. Next pass: z = 0.1, p ≈ 0.525, loss ≈ 0.644 — slightly more confident in the right class.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">w = b = 0.05, p rises 0.500 → 0.525</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Forgetting the bias column. Writing Xw with no column of ones forces the line through the origin, and the fit looks mysteriously bad.</p></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Squared error with a sigmoid output: gradients vanish when the model is confidently wrong.</li>\n<li>Swapping ŷ and y in the loss, which flips every residual sign.</li>\n<li>Believing a network needs no non-linearity. Two linear layers are one linear layer.</li>\n<li>Skipping standardisation, which makes the normal equations ill-conditioned.</li>\n<li>Quoting training accuracy as performance — it measures memorisation.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>\"Fit by normal equations, then by gradient descent, and compare\" is the classic 8–10 mark question. A forward-and-backward pass earns marks per partial derivative shown.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Pipeline: data as matrix → model as function → loss → optimization → trained model.</li>\n<li>Linear regression: squared error, solved by XᵀXw = Xᵀy or by descent.</li>\n<li>Logistic regression: sigmoid output, cross-entropy loss, gradient (p − y)·x, no closed form.</li>\n<li>A layer = matrix product + bias + non-linearity; without it depth collapses.</li>\n<li>Backprop = the chain rule, layer by layer, storing intermediates.</li>\n<li>Four strands, four jobs: represent, measure sensitivity, decide a step, quantify doubt.</li>\n<li>Check every gradient's shape against its parameter's shape.</li>\n<li>NaN loss: log of zero or a step too big. Overfitting: train down, validation up.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Fit y = c + d·t to (0, 1) and (1, 3) using the normal equations.", "a": "<p>Rows (1,0), (1,1): Σ1 = 2, Σt = 1, Σt² = 1, so XᵀX = [[2,1],[1,1]] and Xᵀy = (4, 3).</p>\n<p>2c + d = 4 and c + d = 3 give c = 1 and d = 2. Predictions are exact: two points determine a line.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Evaluate the sigmoid at z = 0, 2 and −2, and explain the output in a classifier.", "a": "<p>σ(0) = 0.5, σ(2) = 1/(1+0.1353) ≈ 0.881, σ(−2) ≈ 0.119, and σ(−z) = 1 − σ(z).</p>\n<p>The output is the estimated probability that the label is 1; it never reaches 0 or 1 exactly.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "For x = 2, y = 1, w = 0, b = 0 and η = 0.1, do one logistic gradient-descent step and report the new loss.", "a": "<p>Forward: z = 0, p = 0.5, loss = 0.693. Gradients: (0.5 − 1)(2) = −1 for w, −0.5 for b.</p>\n<p>Update: w = 0.1, b = 0.05. New z = 0.25, p ≈ 0.562, loss ≈ 0.576.</p>\n<p>The weight step scaled with x while the bias step did not — one reason standardisation matters.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Compute the cross-entropy loss for y = 1 at p = 0.99, 0.5 and 0.01, and compare with squared error.", "a": "<p>With y = 1 the loss is −log p: 0.010, 0.693 and 4.605, while squared error gives 0.00005, 0.25 and 0.98 — capped near 1 even when confidently wrong.</p>\n<p>Cross-entropy grows without bound as p → 0, so the gradient stays large where the model is badly wrong.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "A network has x = 1, w₁ = 2, b₁ = −1, w₂ = 3, b₂ = 0, target 5, ReLU hidden unit, loss ½(ŷ−y)². Do one forward and backward pass, then one update with η = 0.1.", "a": "<p>Forward: z₁ = 1, h = 1, ŷ = 3, L = 2. Backward: dL/dŷ = −2, so dL/dw₂ = dL/db₂ = −2 and dL/dh = −6; ReLU is active, so dL/dw₁ = dL/db₁ = −6.</p>\n<p>Update: w₁ = 2.6, b₁ = −0.4, w₂ = 3.2, b₂ = 0.2, giving h = 2.2, ŷ = 7.24, L ≈ 2.51.</p>\n<p>The loss rose because η = 0.1 overshot; with η = 0.02 the new loss is about 0.013.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "For a two-layer network with 3 inputs, 4 hidden units and 1 output, give the shape of every parameter and of the forward pass, and say which module each step comes from.", "a": "<p>x is 3×1, W₁ is 4×3, b₁ is 4×1, so W₁x + b₁ is 4×1 — legal because inner dimensions match (M1). h = σ(W₁x + b₁) stays 4×1.</p>\n<p>W₂ is 1×4 and b₂ is 1×1, so ŷ = σ(W₂h + b₂) is 1×1: a probability.</p>\n<p>Sources: shapes M1, loss and gradients M3, the update M4, ŷ as probability M5. Check that dL/dW₁ has the shape of W₁ (4×3); a 3×4 answer means something got transposed.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/MTAI-MathematicsForAI/MTAI-M06.md"}];
